// Class 11 Physics Unit V: Motion of System of Particles and Rigid Body (06 Marks)
// Official JKBOSE / CBSE / NCERT Syllabus Alignment
// Gold Standard Reference Textbook & 3-Tab Architecture (Pradeep's / S.L. Arora Standard)

export const c11Phy5HtmlOverview = "<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 16px;\">\n\n  <!-- CHAPTER TITLE BANNER -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #AB47BC; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px; text-align: center;\">\n    <h1 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 24px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Unit V: Motion of System of Particles and Rigid Body (06 Marks)\n    </h1>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 0; font-size: 15px; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Comprehensive Reference Textbook Guide &bull; JKBOSE / CBSE Official Syllabus Alignment\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- QUICK GLOSSARY & BASIC DEFINITIONS CARD -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      ⚡ Quick Glossary & Fundamental Operational Quantities\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; border-radius: 4px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Centre of Mass (CM):</b> Unique spatial point where the entire mass of a system may be considered concentrated. Vector: \u000bec{R}_{\text{cm}} = \frac{1}{M}sum m_i \u000bec{r}_i. SI Unit: <b>meter (m)</b>.\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; border-radius: 4px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Torque (&tau;):</b> Rotational moment of a force; \u000bec{\tau} = \u000bec{r} \times \u000bec{F} = r F sin\theta,hat{n}. SI Unit: <b>N m</b> &bull; Dimensions: <b>[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</b>.\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; border-radius: 4px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Angular Momentum (L):</b> Moment of linear momentum; \u000bec{L} = \u000bec{r} \times \u000bec{p} = I\u000bec{omega}. SI Unit: <b>J s (or kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-1</sup>)</b> &bull; Dimensions: <b>[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]</b>.\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; border-radius: 4px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Moment of Inertia (I):</b> Rotational inertia resisting angular acceleration; I = sum m_i r_i<sup>2</sup> = M k<sup>2</sup>. SI Unit: <b>kg m<sup>2</sup></b> &bull; Dimensions: <b>[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]</b>.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 1 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #AB47BC; border-bottom: 2px solid #AB47BC; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    1. Centre of Mass of a Two-Particle System & N-Particle Systems\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    Up to this point, classical mechanics has treated material bodies as idealized point particles. However, real physical objects possess finite dimensions and are composed of countless interacting constituent particles. When a cricket bat or a wrench is tossed through the air, different points of the object execute intricate, dizzying trajectories. Yet, remarkable simplicity emerges: there exists one unique spatial point within or associated with the body that travels along a clean, simple parabolic trajectory under gravity, moving precisely as if the entire mass of the object were concentrated there and all external forces were applied directly to it. This privileged point is the <b>Centre of Mass (CM)</b>.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #AB47BC; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #AB47BC; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 16px;\">Centre of Mass (CM)</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">The centre of mass of a system of particles is defined as that single point which moves in such a way that the total mass of the system times the acceleration of this point equals the vector sum of all external forces acting on the system.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #CE93D8; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">meter (m)</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #CE93D8; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Two-Particle System Formulation:</b><br>\n    Consider two particles of masses m_1 and m_2 located at position vectors \u000bec{r}_1 and \u000bec{r}_2 with respect to an arbitrary origin O.<br>\n    The position vector \u000bec{R}_{\text{cm}} of their centre of mass is defined as the weighted average:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; margin: 12px 0;\">\n    \u000bec{R}_{\text{cm}} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 \u000bec{r}_1 + m_2 \u000bec{r}_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 \u000bec{r}_1 + m_2 \u000bec{r}_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">M</span>\n  </span> quad \text{where } M = m_1 + m_2\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Cartesian Coordinates of Centre of Mass:</b><br>\n    In three-dimensional space with particle coordinates (x_1, y_1, z_1) and (x_2, y_2, z_2):\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 10px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 12px 0; font-size: 15.5px; color: #CE93D8;\">\n    <b>X_{\text{cm}} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 x_1 + m_2 x_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>, quad Y_{\text{cm}} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 y_1 + m_2 y_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>, quad Z_{\text{cm}} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 z_1 + m_2 z_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span></b>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(14, 165, 233, 0.06); border-left: 4px solid #0EA5E9; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #38BDF8; font-size: 18px;\">💡</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #38BDF8; font-size: 15px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">REAL-WORLD INTUITION &bull; The Seesaw Balance Point</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">If two children sit on a seesaw, the balance fulcrum must be placed closer to the heavier child. The centre of mass of two bodies always lies along the line connecting them, dividing the distance inversely in the ratio of their masses: m_1 r_1 = m_2 r_2. If m_1 = m_2, the CM lies exactly midway.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Extension to an N-Particle System:</b><br>\n    For a system consisting of N discrete particles of masses m_1, m_2, dots, m_N with position vectors \u000bec{r}_1, \u000bec{r}_2, dots, \u000bec{r}_N:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin: 12px 0;\">\n    \u000bec{R}_{\text{cm}} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">sum_{i=1}<sup>N</sup> m_i \u000bec{r}_i</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">sum_{i=1}<sup>N</sup> m_i</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">M</span>\n  </span> sum_{i=1}<sup>N</sup> m_i \u000bec{r}_i\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #AB47BC; border-bottom: 2px solid #AB47BC; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    2. Momentum Conservation & Motion of the Centre of Mass\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    Differentiating the position vector of the centre of mass \u000bec{R}_{\text{cm}} with respect to time t yields the <b>velocity of the centre of mass (\u000bec{V}_{\text{cm}})</b>:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin: 12px 0;\">\n    \u000bec{V}_{\text{cm}} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d\u000bec{R}_{\text{cm}}</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">M</span>\n  </span> sum_{i=1}<sup>N</sup> m_i <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d\u000bec{r}_i</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">M</span>\n  </span> sum_{i=1}<sup>N</sup> m_i \u000bec{v}_i = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">\u000bec{P}_{\text{total}}</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">M</span>\n  </span>\n  </div>\n  <p>\n    Cross-multiplying by total mass M reveals a profound physical law:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #CE93D8; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; margin: 8px 0;\">\n    mathbf{\u000bec{P}_{\text{total}} = M \u000bec{V}_{\text{cm}}}\n  </div>\n  <p>\n    <i>The total linear momentum of a multi-particle system equals the total mass of the system multiplied by the velocity of its centre of mass.</i>\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Acceleration of the Centre of Mass & Equation of Motion:</b><br>\n    Differentiating velocity with respect to time:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin: 10px 0;\">\n    \u000bec{A}_{\text{cm}} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d\u000bec{V}_{\text{cm}}</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">M</span>\n  </span> sum_{i=1}<sup>N</sup> m_i \u000bec{a}_i implies M \u000bec{A}_{\text{cm}} = sum_{i=1}<sup>N</sup> \u000bec{F}_i\n  </div>\n  <p>\n    The force \u000bec{F}_i on the i{\text{th}} particle consists of external forces \u000bec{F}_{i,\text{ext}} and internal mutual forces \u000bec{F}_{ij} exerted by other particles. By Newton's Third Law, internal forces occur in equal and opposite pairs (\u000bec{F}_{ij} = -\u000bec{F}_{ji}), so their vector sum over the entire system cancels out identically: sum \u000bec{F}_{\text{int}} = \u000bec{0}.\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 14px 0; font-size: 17px; color: #76FF03;\">\n    <b>\u000bec{F}_{\text{ext}} = M \u000bec{A}_{\text{cm}} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d\u000bec{P}_{\text{total}}</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span></b>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>The Principle of Conservation of Centre of Mass Motion:</b><br>\n    If the net external force acting on a system is zero (\u000bec{F}_{\text{ext}} = \u000bec{0}):\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin: 10px 0;\">\n    \u000bec{A}_{\text{cm}} = \u000bec{0} implies \u000bec{V}_{\text{cm}} = \text{Constant Vector}\n  </div>\n  <p>\n    <i>In the absence of net external forces, internal forces can never accelerate or alter the trajectory of the centre of mass.</i>\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(239, 68, 68, 0.08); border: 1.5px solid #EF4444; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #F87171; font-size: 18px;\">⚠️</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #F87171; font-size: 14.5px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">EXAM TRAP & COMMON MISCONCEPTION: Exploding Projectile in Mid-Air</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FECACA; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;\">A shell fired from a cannon follows a parabolic trajectory under gravity. If it explodes mid-flight into dozens of fragments, the chemical explosive forces are purely internal. As long as gravity remains the sole external force, the centre of mass of all the scattered fragments continues following the exact same unbroken parabolic path to the ground!</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #AB47BC; border-bottom: 2px solid #AB47BC; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    3. Centre of Mass of a Rigid Body & Uniform Rod\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    A <b>rigid body</b> is a physical body in which the spatial distance between any pair of constituent particles remains strictly constant regardless of the magnitude of external forces applied to it. In continuous rigid bodies, the summation over discrete masses is replaced by a continuous calculus volume integral over infinitesimal mass elements dm:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin: 12px 0;\">\n    \u000bec{R}_{\text{cm}} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">M</span>\n  </span> int \u000bec{r} , dm\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 15.5px;\">Derivation of Centre of Mass of a Uniform Thin Rod</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Coordinate & Mass Density Setup</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Consider a uniform thin rod of mass M and length L lying along the x-axis with one end at the origin (x = 0) and the other end at x = L.\n          <br>Linear mass density (mass per unit length) is constant: lambda = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">M</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span>.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Infinitesimal Element dm</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Take an infinitesimal element of length dx at a distance x from the origin:\n          <br>dm = lambda , dx = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">M</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span>\right) dx.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Calculus Integration</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">By definition of centre of mass:\n          <br>X_{cm} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">M</span>\n  </span> int<sub>0</sub><sup>L</sup> x , dm = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">M</span>\n  </span> int<sub>0</sub><sup>L</sup> x left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">M</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span>\right) dx = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> int<sub>0</sub><sup>L</sup> x , dx.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Evaluate Definite Integral</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">X_{cm} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> left[ <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">x<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span> \right]<sub>0</sub><sup>L</sup> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> left( <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">L<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span> \right) = mathbf{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">L</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span>}.\n          <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; margin-top: 6px;\">\n            X_{cm} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">L</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span> quad \text{(Geometric Midpoint)}\n          </div>\n          <i>For any homogeneous, symmetric body, the centre of mass coincides with its geometric center of symmetry.</i></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Centre of Mass vs Centre of Gravity:</b>\n  </p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Centre of Mass (CM):</b> The point where the total mass of the body is concentrated; determined purely by mass distribution, completely independent of gravity.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Centre of Gravity (CG):</b> The point through which the resultant gravitational force (weight) acts; the point about which the net torque due to gravity vanishes.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Equivalence Condition:</b> In a uniform gravitational field (such as laboratory scale where \u000bec{g} is constant across the body), <b>CM and CG coincide exactly</b>. In non-uniform fields (such as a skyscraper or a satellite in orbit), CG shifts slightly below CM.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #AB47BC; border-bottom: 2px solid #AB47BC; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    4. Moment of a Force (Torque) & Angular Momentum\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    Just as a linear force causes translational acceleration along a line, the turning or twisting effect of a force about a fixed axis causes rotational acceleration. This rotational analog of force is termed the <b>Moment of a Force</b> or <b>Torque (\u000bec{\tau})</b>.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #AB47BC; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #AB47BC; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 16px;\">Torque or Moment of a Force (&tau;)</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Torque about a chosen pivot point is defined as the vector (cross) product of the position vector of the point of application of the force from the pivot and the applied force vector.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #CE93D8; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">Newton meter (N m)</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #CE93D8; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; margin: 12px 0;\">\n    \u000bec{\tau} = \u000bec{r} \times \u000bec{F} = (r F sin\theta) hat{n} = F cdot d_{perp}\n  </div>\n  <p>\n    where d_{perp} = rsin\theta is the <b>moment arm (lever arm)</b>, representing the shortest perpendicular distance from the axis of rotation to the line of action of the force. The direction of \u000bec{\tau} is given by the <b>Right-Hand Thumb Rule</b>: curl the fingers from \u000bec{r} toward \u000bec{F}; the outstretched thumb points in the direction of torque.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(14, 165, 233, 0.06); border-left: 4px solid #0EA5E9; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #38BDF8; font-size: 18px;\">💡</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #38BDF8; font-size: 15px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">REAL-WORLD INTUITION &bull; Opening a Heavy Door</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">To open a door easily, the handle is positioned at the outer edge furthest from the hinges (maximizing r), and you push perpendicular to the door face (&theta; = 90&deg;, maximizing sin &theta;). Pushing near the hinges requires immense force, and pushing parallel to the door edge produces zero torque (&theta; = 0&deg;).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Couple & Moment of a Couple:</b><br>\n    A <b>couple</b> consists of two equal and opposite parallel forces whose lines of action do not coincide (e.g. turning a steering wheel or opening a water tap). The net translational force of a couple is zero (sum \u000bec{F} = \u000bec{0}), so it causes <b>pure rotation without translation</b>. The torque of a couple equals the magnitude of one force multiplied by the perpendicular distance between their lines of action: \tau_{\text{couple}} = F \times d.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #AB47BC; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #AB47BC; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 16px;\">Angular Momentum (L)</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">The angular momentum of a particle about a chosen point is defined as the moment of its linear momentum, given mathematically as the vector product of position vector and linear momentum vector.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #CE93D8; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">Joule second (J s = kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-1</sup>)</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #CE93D8; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; margin: 12px 0;\">\n    \u000bec{L} = \u000bec{r} \times \u000bec{p} = \u000bec{r} \times (m\u000bec{v}) = (r p sin\theta) hat{n} = I \u000bec{omega}\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 15.5px;\">Fundamental Relation Between Torque and Angular Momentum</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Definition of Angular Momentum</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">\u000bec{L} = \u000bec{r} \times \u000bec{p}.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Time Derivative</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Differentiating with respect to time t using the vector product rule:\n          <br><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>L</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>(\u000bec{r} \times \u000bec{p}) = left( <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>r</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> \times \u000bec{p} \right) + left( \u000bec{r} \times <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>p</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> \right).</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Evaluate First Cross Product</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Since (d<b>r</b> / dt) = <b>v</b> and <b>p</b> = m<b>v</b>:\n          <br><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>r</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> \times \u000bec{p} = \u000bec{v} \times (m\u000bec{v}) = m(\u000bec{v} \times \u000bec{v}) = \u000bec{0} (since cross product of any vector with itself vanishes).</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Substitute Second Law for Second Term</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">By Newton's Second Law: (d<b>p</b> / dt) = <b>F</b>.\n          <br>Therefore: \u000bec{r} \times <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>p</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = \u000bec{r} \times \u000bec{F} = \u000bec{\tau}.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">5</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Final Master Relation</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\"><div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n          \u000bec{\tau} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>L</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>\n        </div>\n        <i>Torque is the time rate of change of angular momentum, perfectly mirroring Newton's Second Law <b>F</b> = d<b>p</b>/dt in rotational dynamics.</i></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 5 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #AB47BC; border-bottom: 2px solid #AB47BC; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    5. Law of Conservation of Angular Momentum & Applications\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    The Principle of Conservation of Angular Momentum is a universal conservation law of classical and modern physics.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #AB47BC; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #AB47BC; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 16px;\">Law of Conservation of Angular Momentum</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">If the net external torque acting on a rotating system about an axis is identically zero, the total vector angular momentum of the system remains strictly constant over time.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #CE93D8; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">—</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #CE93D8; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">—</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; margin: 12px 0;\">\n    \u000bec{\tau}_{\text{ext}} = \u000bec{0} implies <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d\u000bec{L}</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = \u000bec{0} implies \u000bec{L} = \text{Constant Vector} implies I_1 omega_1 = I_2 omega_2\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Major Real-World Applications:</b>\n  </p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">1. Ballet Dancer & Figure Skater:</b> A spinning ice skater begins spinning slowly with arms and legs outstretched (large moment of inertia I_1). When she draws her arms and legs close to her chest, her moment of inertia drops sharply to I_2 < I_1. To conserve angular momentum (I_1omega_1 = I_2omega_2), her angular speed omega_2 increases dramatically, producing a rapid blur spin.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">2. Acrobat & Olympic Diver:</b> A diver jumping off a high springboard tucks their body tightly into a ball (fetal tuck), minimizing I and spinning rapidly through multiple somersaults. Just before entering the water, the diver stretches arms and legs out, increasing I, which slows the rotation rate to ensure clean vertical entry.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">3. Planetary Motion (Kepler's Second Law):</b> The gravitational pull of the Sun on a planet acts directly along the radial line joining them, generating zero torque (\u000bec{\tau} = \u000bec{r} \times \u000bec{F}_g = \u000bec{0}). Therefore, orbital angular momentum is conserved: L = m r<sup>2</sup> omega = \text{constant}. The areal velocity is constant: \frac{dA}{dt} = \frac{L}{2m} = \text{constant}. When closer to the Sun at perihelion (r decreases), orbital speed increases (v increases).</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 6 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #AB47BC; border-bottom: 2px solid #AB47BC; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    6. Equilibrium of Rigid Bodies & The Principle of Moments\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    Unlike point particles which only require zero net force for equilibrium, an extended rigid body can experience both translation and rotation. Complete mechanical equilibrium requires satisfying two simultaneous vector criteria:\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>1. First Condition of Equilibrium (Translational Equilibrium):</b><br>\n    The vector sum of all external forces acting on the rigid body must be zero:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #CE93D8; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin: 8px 0;\">\n    sum \u000bec{F}_{\text{ext}} = \u000bec{0} quad Longleftrightarrow quad sum F_x = 0, ; sum F_y = 0, ; sum F_z = 0\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>2. Second Condition of Equilibrium (Rotational Equilibrium):</b><br>\n    The vector sum of all external torques acting on the rigid body about any chosen point must be zero:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #CE93D8; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin: 8px 0;\">\n    sum \u000bec{\tau}_{\text{ext}} = \u000bec{0} quad Longleftrightarrow quad sum \tau_{\text{clockwise}} = sum \tau_{\text{anticlockwise}}\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>The Principle of Moments & Mechanical Advantage:</b><br>\n    For a lever pivoted at fulcrum F with load W at distance d_1 (load arm) and effort P at distance d_2 (effort arm):\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 10px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 12px 0; font-size: 15.5px; color: #76FF03;\">\n    <b>\text{Load} \times \text{Load Arm} = \text{Effort} \times \text{Effort Arm} implies \text{Mechanical Advantage (MA)} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">\text{Load}</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\text{Effort}</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">\text{Effort Arm}</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\text{Load Arm}</span>\n  </span></b>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 7 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #AB47BC; border-bottom: 2px solid #AB47BC; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    7. Rigid Body Rotation & Equations of Rotational Motion\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    When a rigid body rotates about a fixed axis, every constituent particle of the body moves in a circular path centered on the axis with the exact same angular velocity omega and angular acceleration alpha, although particles at different distances r have different linear speeds (v = romega).\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Equations of Rotational Motion with Constant Angular Acceleration (&alpha;):</b><br>\n    These equations are the exact rotational counterparts of the linear kinematic equations:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 14px 0; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 16px; color: #76FF03;\">\n    <b>1. &omega; = &omega;<sub>0</sub> + &alpha; t</b><br>\n    <b>2. &theta; = &omega;<sub>0</sub> t + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span> &alpha; t<sup>2</sup></b><br>\n    <b>3. &omega;<sup>2</sup> - &omega;<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> = 2 &alpha; &theta;</b><br>\n    <b>4. &theta;<sub>n</sub> = &omega;<sub>0</sub> + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">&alpha;</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span> (2n - 1) quad \text{(Angular displacement in } n{\text{th}} \text{ second)}</b>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Rotational Kinetic Energy, Work & Power:</b><br>\n    &bull; <b>Rotational Kinetic Energy:</b> K_{\text{rot}} = sum \frac{1}{2}m_i v_i<sup>2</sup> = sum \frac{1}{2}m_i (r_iomega)<sup>2</sup> = \frac{1}{2}left(sum m_i r_i<sup>2</sup>\right)omega<sup>2</sup> = mathbf{\frac{1}{2} I omega<sup>2</sup>}.<br>\n    &bull; <b>Rotational Work Done:</b> dW = \tau , d\theta implies mathbf{W = int_{\theta_1}<sup>\theta_2</sup> \tau , d\theta}.<br>\n    &bull; <b>Rotational Power:</b> P = \frac{dW}{dt} = \tau , \frac{d\theta}{dt} = mathbf{\tau omega}.\n  </p>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 8 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #AB47BC; border-bottom: 2px solid #AB47BC; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    8. Comparison of Linear (Translational) & Rotational Motions\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    There exists a complete, elegant one-to-one mathematical isomorphism between the operational physical quantities of translational mechanics and rotational dynamics:\n  </p>\n\n  <!-- COMPARISON TABLE -->\n  <div style=\"overflow-x: auto; margin: 20px 0;\">\n    <table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); font-size: 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3);\">\n      <thead>\n        <tr style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.15); border-bottom: 2px solid #AB47BC;\">\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; color: #AB47BC; text-align: left;\">Translational Motion</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; color: #AB47BC; text-align: left;\">Rotational Motion</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; color: #AB47BC; text-align: left;\">Connecting Relation</th>\n        </tr>\n      </thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Linear Displacement: s</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Angular Displacement: \theta</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">s = r \theta</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Linear Velocity: v = ds/dt</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Angular Velocity: omega = d\theta/dt</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">\u000bec{v} = \u000bec{omega} \times \u000bec{r} (v = romega)</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Linear Accel: a = dv/dt</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Angular Accel: alpha = domega/dt</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">a_t = r alpha</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Mass (Inertia): m</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Moment of Inertia: I</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">I = sum m_i r_i<sup>2</sup> = M k<sup>2</sup></td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Force: \u000bec{F} = m \u000bec{a}</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Torque: \u000bec{\tau} = I \u000bec{alpha}</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">\u000bec{\tau} = \u000bec{r} \times \u000bec{F}</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Linear Momentum: \u000bec{p} = m \u000bec{v}</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Angular Momentum: \u000bec{L} = I \u000bec{omega}</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">\u000bec{L} = \u000bec{r} \times \u000bec{p}</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Newton's 2nd: \u000bec{F} = d\u000bec{p}/dt</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Rotational 2nd: \u000bec{\tau} = d\u000bec{L}/dt</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">—</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Work: W = int F , ds</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Work: W = int \tau , d\theta</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">—</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Kinetic Energy: K = \frac{1}{2}m v<sup>2</sup></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Kinetic Energy: K = \frac{1}{2}I omega<sup>2</sup></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">—</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Power: P = \u000bec{F} cdot \u000bec{v}</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Power: P = \tau omega</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">—</td>\n        </tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 9 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #AB47BC; border-bottom: 2px solid #AB47BC; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    9. Moment of Inertia, Radius of Gyration & Geometrical Lookup Values\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    In translational mechanics, the inertial mass m of an object is a fixed scalar constant that purely resists linear acceleration. In rotational dynamics, however, resistance to angular acceleration depends not only on total mass M, but critically on <b>how that mass is distributed in space relative to the chosen axis of rotation</b>. A spinning fly-wheel with heavy perimeter rims has vastly greater rotational inertia than a compact sphere of identical total mass.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #AB47BC; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #AB47BC; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 16px;\">Moment of Inertia (I)</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">The moment of inertia of a rigid body about a given axis of rotation is defined as the sum of the products of the mass of each constituent particle and the square of its perpendicular distance from the axis of rotation.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #CE93D8; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">kg m<sup>2</sup></code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #CE93D8; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; margin: 12px 0;\">\n    I = sum_{i=1}<sup>N</sup> m_i r_i<sup>2</sup> = int r<sup>2</sup> , dm\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Factors Affecting Moment of Inertia:</b><br>\n    1. Total mass of the body.<br>\n    2. Geometrical shape and dimensions of the body.<br>\n    3. Spatial distribution of mass relative to the axis of rotation.<br>\n    4. Position and orientation of the axis of rotation.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #AB47BC; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #AB47BC; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 16px;\">Radius of Gyration (k)</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">The radius of gyration of a body about a given axis of rotation is defined as the perpendicular distance from the axis at which the entire mass of the body could be concentrated as a single point particle without altering its moment of inertia about that axis.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #CE93D8; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">meter (m)</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #CE93D8; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; margin: 12px 0;\">\n    I = M k<sup>2</sup> implies mathbf{k = sqrt{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">I</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">M</span>\n  </span>}}\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Master Lookup Table: Values of Moments of Inertia for Simple Geometrical Objects:</b><br>\n    <i>(As specified in the official syllabus, derivations are not required; standard values must be mastered for problem-solving):</i>\n  </p>\n\n  <!-- MOMENT OF INERTIA LOOKUP TABLE -->\n  <div style=\"overflow-x: auto; margin: 20px 0;\">\n    <table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); font-size: 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3);\">\n      <thead>\n        <tr style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.15); border-bottom: 2px solid #AB47BC;\">\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; color: #AB47BC; text-align: left;\">Body & Geometry</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; color: #AB47BC; text-align: left;\">Axis of Rotation</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; color: #AB47BC; text-align: left;\">Moment of Inertia (I)</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; color: #AB47BC; text-align: left;\">Radius of Gyration (k)</th>\n        </tr>\n      </thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CBD5E1; font-weight: bold;\">Thin Circular Ring (Mass M, Radius R)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Central axis perpendicular to plane</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #76FF03; font-weight: bold;\">M R<sup>2</sup></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">R</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CBD5E1; font-weight: bold;\">Thin Circular Ring (Mass M, Radius R)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Diameter in the plane of the ring</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #76FF03; font-weight: bold;\"><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span> M R<sup>2</sup></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">R / sqrt{2}</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CBD5E1; font-weight: bold;\">Uniform Circular Disc (Mass M, Radius R)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Central axis perpendicular to plane</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #76FF03; font-weight: bold;\"><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span> M R<sup>2</sup></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">R / sqrt{2}</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CBD5E1; font-weight: bold;\">Uniform Circular Disc (Mass M, Radius R)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Diameter in the plane of the disc</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #76FF03; font-weight: bold;\"><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">4</span>\n  </span> M R<sup>2</sup></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">R / 2</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CBD5E1; font-weight: bold;\">Thin Uniform Rod (Mass M, Length L)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Perpendicular bisector through center</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #76FF03; font-weight: bold;\"><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">12</span>\n  </span> M L<sup>2</sup></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">L / sqrt{12}</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CBD5E1; font-weight: bold;\">Thin Uniform Rod (Mass M, Length L)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Perpendicular axis through one end</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #76FF03; font-weight: bold;\"><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">3</span>\n  </span> M L<sup>2</sup></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">L / sqrt{3}</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CBD5E1; font-weight: bold;\">Solid Cylinder (Mass M, Radius R)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Central longitudinal axis of symmetry</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #76FF03; font-weight: bold;\"><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span> M R<sup>2</sup></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">R / sqrt{2}</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CBD5E1; font-weight: bold;\">Hollow Cylinder (Thin Shell, M, R)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Central longitudinal axis of symmetry</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #76FF03; font-weight: bold;\">M R<sup>2</sup></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">R</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CBD5E1; font-weight: bold;\">Solid Sphere (Mass M, Radius R)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Any diameter passing through center</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #76FF03; font-weight: bold;\"><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">5</span>\n  </span> M R<sup>2</sup></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">sqrt{2/5} , R</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CBD5E1; font-weight: bold;\">Hollow Spherical Shell (Mass M, Radius R)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Any diameter passing through center</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #76FF03; font-weight: bold;\"><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">3</span>\n  </span> M R<sup>2</sup></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #CE93D8;\">sqrt{2/3} , R</td>\n        </tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- MASTER REVISION FORMULA CHEAT SHEET -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #AB47BC; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 15px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">\n      ⚡ Master Revision Formula Cheat Sheet\n    </h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Comprehensive Operational Equations &bull; Unit V: System of Particles & Rotational Motion (06 Marks)\n    </p>\n\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #AB47BC; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Centre of Mass Relations:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          \u000bec{R}_{\text{cm}} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">sum m_i \u000bec{r}_i</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">M</span>\n  </span><br>\n          \u000bec{P}_{\text{total}} = M \u000bec{V}_{\text{cm}}<br>\n          \u000bec{F}_{\text{ext}} = M \u000bec{A}_{\text{cm}}<br>\n          Uniform Rod: X_{\text{cm}} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">L</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span>\n        </span>\n      </div>\n\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #AB47BC; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Torque & Angular Momentum:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          \u000bec{\tau} = \u000bec{r} \times \u000bec{F} = I \u000bec{alpha}<br>\n          \u000bec{L} = \u000bec{r} \times \u000bec{p} = I \u000bec{omega}<br>\n          \u000bec{\tau} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d\u000bec{L}</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span><br>\n          \u000bec{\tau}_{\text{ext}} = \u000bec{0} implies I_1 omega_1 = I_2 omega_2\n        </span>\n      </div>\n\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #AB47BC; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Rotational Kinematics:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          omega = omega_0 + alpha t<br>\n          \theta = omega_0 t + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span> alpha t<sup>2</sup><br>\n          omega<sup>2</sup> - omega<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> = 2 alpha \theta<br>\n          K_{\text{rot}} = \frac{1}{2} I omega<sup>2</sup>, quad P = \tau omega\n        </span>\n      </div>\n\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #AB47BC; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Moment of Inertia (I = M k<sup>2</sup>):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          Ring (center): I = M R<sup>2</sup><br>\n          Disc (center): I = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span> M R<sup>2</sup><br>\n          Solid Sphere: I = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">5</span>\n  </span> M R<sup>2</sup><br>\n          Hollow Sphere: I = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">3</span>\n  </span> M R<sup>2</sup>\n        </span>\n      </div>\n\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #AB47BC; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Rods & Cylinders:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          Rod (center): I = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">12</span>\n  </span> M L<sup>2</sup><br>\n          Rod (end): I = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">3</span>\n  </span> M L<sup>2</sup><br>\n          Solid Cylinder: I = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span> M R<sup>2</sup><br>\n          Hollow Cylinder: I = M R<sup>2</sup>\n        </span>\n      </div>\n\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #AB47BC; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Equilibrium & Moments:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          sum \u000bec{F}_{\text{ext}} = \u000bec{0} quad \text{(Translational)}<br>\n          sum \u000bec{\tau}_{\text{ext}} = \u000bec{0} quad \text{(Rotational)}<br>\n          Principle: \text{Load} \times d_1 = \text{Effort} \times d_2<br>\n          MA = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">d_1</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">\text{Effort Arm}</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\text{Load Arm}</span>\n  </span>\n        </span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n</div>";

export const c11Phy5HtmlSolutions = "<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 16px;\">\n\n  <!-- TITLE BANNER -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #AB47BC; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px; text-align: center;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 22px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">\n      NCERT & Master Solutions Guide (100 Questions)\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 0; font-size: 15px; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Class 11 Physics Unit V: Motion of System of Particles and Rigid Body (06 Marks) &bull; Official Syllabus Coverage\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <!-- SECTION A: VERY SHORT ANSWER (VSA) QUESTIONS (1 MARK EACH) -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.08); border-left: 4px solid #AB47BC; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 25px 0 15px 0; border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      SECTION A: Very Short Answer (VSA) Questions (1 Mark Each) &bull; Q1 to Q40\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 13px;\">\n      Fundamental definitions, dimensional analysis, core formulas, SI units, and instantaneous conceptual facts.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q1 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q1: Define the Centre of Mass (CM) of a system of particles.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> The <b>centre of mass</b> of a system of particles is defined as that unique spatial point at which the entire mass of the system is imagined to be concentrated, such that any external force applied to the system produces the exact same translational acceleration as if applied directly to this single point:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      <b>R</b>_{cm} = \frac{\\sum m_i <b>r</b>_i}{\\sum m_i} = (1 / M)\\sum m_i <b>r</b>_i\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q2 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q2: Does the centre of mass of a body necessarily lie within the material of the body? Give an example.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> <b>No</b>, the centre of mass does not necessarily lie inside the material of the body. For hollow or curved symmetrical objects—such as a uniform circular ring, a hollow sphere, or a hollow cylinder—the centre of mass lies at the geometric centre in empty space where no material exists.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q3 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q3: Write down the mathematical coordinates of the centre of mass for a two-particle system along the x-axis.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> For two masses m_1 and m_2 located at coordinates x_1 and x_2 on the x-axis, the coordinate of the centre of mass is:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      X_{cm} = (m_1 x_1 + m_2 x_2 / m_1 + m_2)\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q4 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q4: If the origin of the coordinate system is chosen at the centre of mass, what is the sum of mass moments &Sigma; m<sub>i</sub> r<sub>i</sub>?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> If the origin is placed at the centre of mass, <b>R</b>_{cm} = <b>0</b>. Therefore, the sum of the mass moments of all particles about the centre of mass is strictly zero:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      \\sum m_i <b>r</b>_i = M <b>R</b>_{cm} = <b>0</b>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q5 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q5: How does the centre of mass divide the straight line joining two particles of masses m<sub>1</sub> and m<sub>2</sub>?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> The centre of mass divides the line segment joining the two masses internally in the <b>inverse ratio of their masses</b>:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      m_1 r_1 = m_2 r_2 &rArr; (r_1 / r_2) = (m_2 / m_1)\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      Hence, the centre of mass always lies closer to the heavier particle.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q6 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q6: Why do internal forces have no effect on the motion of the centre of mass of a system?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> According to <b>Newton&apos;s Third Law of Motion</b>, internal mutual interaction forces between pairs of particles inside a system are strictly equal in magnitude and opposite in direction (<b>F</b>_{ij} = -<b>F</b>_{ji}). Therefore, their vector sum over the entire system identically cancels out to zero (\\sum <b>F</b>_{int} = <b>0</b>).\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q7 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q7: State the law of conservation of linear momentum for a system of particles.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> If the net external force acting on a system of particles is zero (<b>F</b>_{ext} = <b>0</b>), the total linear momentum <b>P</b> of the system remains strictly constant in time:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      <b>P</b> = M <b>V</b>_{cm} = constant vector\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q8 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q8: A bomb at rest explodes in mid-air into several fragments. What is the trajectory of the centre of mass of the fragments?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> Since the explosion is driven solely by <b>internal chemical forces</b> and the only external force is gravity (M<b>g</b>), the centre of mass continues along its original parabolic path uninterrupted, exactly as if no explosion had occurred.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q9 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q9: Where is the centre of mass of a thin uniform rod of length L situated?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> By symmetry and calculus integration (X_{cm} = (1 / L)&int;<sub>0</sub><sup>L</sup> x\\,dx), the centre of mass of a uniform rod lies at its <b>geometric midpoint</b>:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      X_{cm} = (L / 2) from either end.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q10 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q10: Distinguish in one sentence between the Centre of Mass and Centre of Gravity.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> The <b>Centre of Mass</b> depends solely on the distribution of matter, whereas the <b>Centre of Gravity</b> is the point where the net resultant gravitational force acts; they coincide only in a uniform gravitational field.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q11 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q11: Define torque (moment of a force) in vector form.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> The <b>torque</b> <b>&tau;</b> of a force <b>F</b> acting at a point whose position vector with respect to a reference origin is <b>r</b> is given by the cross product:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      <b>&tau;</b> = <b>r</b> &times; <b>F</b> &bull; Magnitude: &tau; = r F \\sin&theta;\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q12 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q12: State the SI unit and dimensional formula of torque.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> \n      <br/>&bull; <b>SI Unit:</b> Newton-metre (<b>N m</b>)\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <b>[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</b>\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q13 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q13: Work and torque have identical dimensional formulas [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]. How are they fundamentally different?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> <b>Work</b> is a scalar quantity defined by a dot product (W = <b>F</b> \\cdot <b>d</b>) with unit Joule (J), whereas <b>torque</b> is an axial vector defined by a cross product (<b>&tau;</b> = <b>r</b> &times; <b>F</b>) measured in Newton-metres (N m), representing rotational turning effect.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q14 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q14: Under what conditions is the torque produced by a force zero?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> Since &tau; = r F \\sin&theta;, torque is zero when:\n      <br/>1. The force F = 0, or\n      <br/>2. The line of action passes through the reference origin (r = 0), or\n      <br/>3. The force is parallel or antiparallel to <b>r</b> (\theta = 0\\circ or 180\\circ).\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q15 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q15: What is a couple in rotational mechanics?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> A <b>couple</b> consists of two equal, opposite, and non-collinear parallel forces acting simultaneously on a rigid body, producing pure rotation without any translational acceleration.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q16 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q16: Write the formula for the moment of a couple.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> The moment of a couple is equal to the product of either force magnitude and the perpendicular distance d (arm of the couple) between their parallel lines of action:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      \\tau_{couple} = F &times; d\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q17 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q17: Why is the handle of a door placed at its outermost edge away from the hinges?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> Because torque &tau; = r F \\sin&theta;. Maximizing the lever arm r produces the required turning torque with the minimum applied manual force F.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q18 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q18: Define angular momentum of a particle in vector form.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> The <b>angular momentum</b> <b>L</b> of a particle of linear momentum <b>p</b> with position vector <b>r</b> relative to an origin is defined as:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      <b>L</b> = <b>r</b> &times; <b>p</b> = m(<b>r</b> &times; <b>v</b>)\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q19 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q19: State the SI unit and dimensional formula of angular momentum.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> \n      <br/>&bull; <b>SI Unit:</b> Joule-second (<b>J s</b>) or <b>kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-1</sup></b>\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <b>[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]</b> (identical to Planck&apos;s constant h)\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q20 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q20: State the fundamental relation between torque and angular momentum.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> The time rate of change of the total angular momentum of a particle or system equals the net external torque acting on it:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      <b>&tau;</b>_{ext} = \frac{d<b>L</b>}{dt}\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q21 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q21: State the Law of Conservation of Angular Momentum.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> If the net external torque acting on a system is zero (<b>&tau;</b>_{ext} = <b>0</b>), the total angular momentum <b>L</b> of the system remains strictly constant:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      <b>L</b> = I <b>\\omega</b> = constant &rArr; I_1 \\omega_1 = I_2 \\omega_2\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q22 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q22: Why does an ice-skater spin faster when she pulls her arms inward?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> By pulling her arms in, mass moves closer to the rotation axis, decreasing her moment of inertia I. To conserve angular momentum (L = I\\omega = constant), her angular velocity \\omega increases proportionally.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q23 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q23: How does a diver execute multiple somersaults before plunging into water?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> The diver tucks the body by curling arms and legs inward, drastically reducing moment of inertia I, which causes the spin speed \\omega to surge according to I_1\\omega_1 = I_2\\omega_2.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q24 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q24: What are the two necessary and sufficient conditions for complete mechanical equilibrium of a rigid body?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> \n      <br/>1. <b>Translational Equilibrium:</b> Net external force must vanish: \\sum <b>F</b>_{ext} = <b>0</b>.\n      <br/>2. <b>Rotational Equilibrium:</b> Net external torque about any point must vanish: \\sum <b>&tau;</b>_{ext} = <b>0</b>.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q25 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q25: State the Principle of Moments for a lever in rotational equilibrium.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> For a lever in rotational equilibrium about a fulcrum:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      Sum of Anticlockwise Moments = Sum of Clockwise Moments &rArr; Load &times; d_1 = Effort &times; d_2\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q26 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q26: What is the mechanical advantage (M.A.) of a lever?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> Mechanical advantage is the ratio of Load to Effort, or Effort Arm to Load Arm:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      M.A. = \frac{Load}{Effort} = (d_2 / d_1) = \frac{Effort Arm}{Load Arm}\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q27 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q27: Write down the three kinematic equations of rotational motion for constant angular acceleration &alpha;.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> \n      <br/>1. \\omega = \\omega_0 + \\alpha t\n      <br/>2. &theta; = \\omega_0 t + (1 / 2)\\alpha t<sup>2</sup>\n      <br/>3. \\omega<sup>2</sup> = \\omega<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> + 2\\alpha &theta;\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q28 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q28: Define Moment of Inertia of a rigid body about a given axis of rotation.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> The <b>moment of inertia</b> I is defined as the sum of the products of each constituent particle&apos;s mass and the square of its perpendicular distance from the rotation axis:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      I = \\sum_{i=1}<sup>n</sup> m_i r_i<sup>2</sup>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q29 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q29: State the SI unit and dimensional formula of moment of inertia.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> \n      <br/>&bull; <b>SI Unit:</b> kilogram metre-squared (<b>kg m<sup>2</sup></b>)\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <b>[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]</b>\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q30 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q30: State the physical significance of moment of inertia.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> Moment of inertia plays the exact same role in rotational motion that mass plays in linear motion—it represents the rotational inertia of the body resisting changes in its rotational state under an applied torque (&tau; = I\\alpha).\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q31 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q31: On what factors does the moment of inertia of a body depend?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> Moment of inertia depends on:\n      <br/>1. Total mass of the body M.\n      <br/>2. Shape and size of the body.\n      <br/>3. Distribution of mass relative to the axis of rotation.\n      <br/>4. Position and orientation of the chosen rotational axis.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q32 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q32: Define Radius of Gyration (k).</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> The <b>radius of gyration</b> k is the effective perpendicular distance from the axis of rotation to a point where, if the entire mass M of the body were concentrated, its moment of inertia would be equal to the actual moment of inertia I:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      I = M k<sup>2</sup> &rArr; k = &radic;{(I / M)}}\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q33 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q33: State the SI unit and dimensional formula of radius of gyration.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> \n      <br/>&bull; <b>SI Unit:</b> metre (<b>m</b>)\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <b>[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]</b>\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q34 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q34: What is the moment of inertia of a thin circular ring of mass M and radius R about its central symmetry axis?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> Since all constituent mass elements are at an identical perpendicular distance R:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      I_{ring} = M R<sup>2</sup> &bull; (k = R)\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q35 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q35: State the moment of inertia of a uniform circular disc of mass M and radius R about its central perpendicular axis.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> \n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      I_{disc} = (1 / 2) M R<sup>2</sup> &bull; (k = (R / &radic;(2)))\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q36 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q36: State the moment of inertia of a thin uniform rod of mass M and length L about an axis through its centre perpendicular to length.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> \n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      I_{rod, cm} = (1 / 12) M L<sup>2</sup> &bull; (k = (L / &radic;(12)))\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q37 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q37: What is the moment of inertia of a thin rod of mass M and length L about an axis through one of its ends?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> \n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      I_{rod, end} = (1 / 3) M L<sup>2</sup>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q38 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q38: State the moment of inertia of a uniform solid sphere of mass M and radius R about its diameter.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> \n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      I_{solid sphere} = (2 / 5) M R<sup>2</sup> &bull; (k = &radic;{(2 / 5)}} R)\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q39 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q39: State the moment of inertia of a thin spherical shell (hollow sphere) about its diameter.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> \n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      I_{hollow sphere} = (2 / 3) M R<sup>2</sup> &bull; (k = &radic;{(2 / 3)}} R)\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q40 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q40: Between a solid cylinder and a hollow cylinder of equal mass and radius, which has a larger moment of inertia about its central axis?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Answer:</b> The <b>hollow cylinder</b> (I = M R<sup>2</sup>) has a larger moment of inertia than the solid cylinder (I = (1 / 2) M R<sup>2</sup>), because all of its mass is concentrated at the maximum possible distance R from the rotational axis.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <!-- SECTION B: SHORT ANSWER (SA) QUESTIONS (2 & 3 MARKS EACH) -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.08); border-left: 4px solid #AB47BC; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 25px 0 15px 0; border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      SECTION B: Short Answer (SA) Questions (2 & 3 Marks Each) &bull; Q41 to Q80\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 13px;\">\n      Mathematical calculations, 2-mark conceptual deductions, coordinate transformations, and rotational dynamics problems.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q41 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q41: Three particles of masses 1 kg, 2 kg, and 3 kg are placed at the vertices of an equilateral triangle of side 1 m. Find the coordinates of their centre of mass.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> Place m_1 = 1 kg at (0, 0), m_2 = 2 kg at (1, 0), and m_3 = 3 kg at (0.5, \frac{&radic;(3)}{2}}).\n      <br/>Total mass: M = 1 + 2 + 3 = 6 kg.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      X_{cm} = (1(0) + 2(1) + 3(0.5) / 6) = (3.5 / 6) = (7 / 12) &approx; 0.583 m<br/>\n      Y_{cm} = \frac{1(0) + 2(0) + 3(&radic;(3)/2)}{6} = \frac{3&radic;(3)}{12} = \frac{&radic;(3)}{4} &approx; 0.433 m\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      <b>Centre of Mass:</b> ((7 / 12) m, \frac{&radic;(3)}{4} m).\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q42 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q42: Two bodies of masses 10 kg and 30 kg are placed on the x-axis at x = 0 and x = 2 m. If the 10 kg mass is moved 0.5 m to the right, by what distance should the 30 kg mass be moved to keep the centre of mass fixed?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> For the centre of mass position to remain unchanged:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      &Delta;X_{cm} = 0 &rArr; m_1 &Delta;x_1 + m_2 &Delta;x_2 = 0<br/>\n      10(+0.5) + 30(&Delta;x_2) = 0 &rArr; 5 + 30 &Delta;x_2 = 0<br/>\n      &Delta;x_2 = -(5 / 30) = -(1 / 6) &approx; -0.167 m\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      The 30 kg mass must be shifted by <b>(1 / 6) m (or ~16.7 cm) to the left</b>.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q43 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q43: Four particles of masses m, 2m, 3m, and 4m are located at the corners of a square of side L: (0,0), (L,0), (L,L), and (0,L). Find the coordinates of the centre of mass.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> Total mass: M = m + 2m + 3m + 4m = 10m.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      X_{cm} = (m(0) + 2m(L) + 3m(L) + 4m(0) / 10m) = (5mL / 10m) = 0.5 L<br/>\n      Y_{cm} = (m(0) + 2m(0) + 3m(L) + 4m(L) / 10m) = (7mL / 10m) = 0.7 L\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      <b>Coordinates of CM:</b> (0.5 L, 0.7 L).\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q44 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q44: From a uniform circular disc of radius R, a circular hole of radius R/2 is cut out touching the edge. Find the centre of mass of the remaining portion.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> Let original disc have surface mass density sigma, mass M = sigma pi R<sup>2</sup>, centre at (0, 0).\n      <br/>Removed hole has area pi (R/2)<sup>2</sup> = (&pi; R<sup>2</sup> / 4), mass m = (M / 4), centre at (R/2, 0).\n      <br/>Using negative mass concept:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      X_{cm} = (M(0) - m(R/2) / M - m) = (- (M/4)(R/2) / M - M/4) = (- M R / 8 / 3M / 4) = -(R / 6)\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      The centre of mass shifts by <b>R/6 away from the hole</b> along the line of symmetry: (-R/6, 0).\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q45 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q45: A 60 kg man and a 40 kg boy stand at opposite ends of a stationary boat of mass 100 kg and length 4 m on still, frictionless water. If they walk across and exchange positions, how much does the boat move?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> Since no horizontal external force acts on the system (boat + man + boy), the centre of mass of the entire system remains at rest: &Delta;X_{cm} = 0.\n      <br/>Let the boat shift by distance x relative to the water.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      m_{man}(L - x) + m_{boy}(-L - x) + M_{boat}(-x) = 0<br/>\n      60(4 - x) + 40(-4 - x) - 100x = 0<br/>\n      240 - 60x - 160 - 40x - 100x = 0 &rArr; 80 - 200x = 0<br/>\n      x = (80 / 200) = 0.4 m = 40 cm\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      The boat shifts by <b>0.4 m (40 cm)</b> towards the side where the heavier man originally stood.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q46 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q46: Two particles of masses 2 kg and 3 kg have velocity vectors (2&icirc; + 3&jcirc;) m/s and (&icirc; - &jcirc;) m/s. Calculate the velocity of their centre of mass.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b>\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      <b>V</b>_{cm} = \frac{m_1 <b>v</b>_1 + m_2 <b>v</b>_2}{m_1 + m_2} = \frac{2(2\\hat{i} + 3\\hat{j}) + 3(\\hat{i} - \\hat{j})}{2 + 3}<br/>\n      = \frac{(4\\hat{i} + 6\\hat{j}) + (3\\hat{i} - 3\\hat{j})}{5} = \frac{7\\hat{i} + 3\\hat{j}}{5} = 1.4\\hat{i} + 0.6\\hat{j} m/s\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q47 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q47: Why do internal forces fail to alter the linear momentum of an isolated system? Illustrate with Newton&apos;s third law.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> For every internal force <b>F</b>_{ij} that particle j exerts on particle i, Newton&apos;s third law requires an equal and opposite force <b>F</b>_{ji} = -<b>F</b>_{ij} exerted by particle i on particle j. Summing over all pairs:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      \\sum <b>F</b>_{int} = \\sum_{i \\ne j} <b>F</b>_{ij} = <b>0</b> &rArr; rac{d<b>P</b>}{dt} = <b>F</b>_{ext} = <b>0</b>\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      Therefore, internal interactions can redistribute momentum among parts, but the total system momentum <b>P</b> remains strictly invariant.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q48 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q48: Differentiate between an idealized rigid body and a real elastic body.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> \n      <br/>&bull; <b>Rigid Body:</b> An idealized solid body in which the relative distance between any pair of constituent particles remains perfectly invariant, regardless of the magnitude of external forces applied.\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Elastic Body:</b> A real body whose constituent particles undergo measurable relative displacement (deformation) under external deforming stress, returning to original configuration when deforming forces are removed.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q49 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q49: A mechanic applies a force of 50 N at an angle of 60&deg; to a wrench of length 30 cm. Calculate the torque generated about the bolt.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> Given: F = 50 N, r = 30 cm = 0.3 m, &theta; = 60\\circ.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      &tau; = r F \\sin&theta; = 0.3 &times; 50 &times; \\sin(60\\circ) = 15 &times; rac{&radic;(3)}{2} = 7.5&radic;(3) &approx; 12.99 N m\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q50 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q50: A force F = (2&icirc; - 3&jcirc; + 4k̂) N acts at the position vector r = (3&icirc; + 2&jcirc; + 3k̂) m. Compute the torque vector about the origin.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> Torque is the determinant vector cross product:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      <b>&tau;</b> = <b>r</b> &times; <b>F</b> = \\begin{vmatrix} \\hat{i} & \\hat{j} & \\hat{k} \\\\ 3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 4 \\end{vmatrix}<br/>\n      = \\hat{i}[ (2)(4) - (3)(-3) ] - \\hat{j}[ (3)(4) - (3)(2) ] + \\hat{k}[ (3)(-3) - (2)(2) ]<br/>\n      = \\hat{i}(8 + 9) - \\hat{j}(12 - 6) + \\hat{k}(-9 - 4) = 17\\hat{i} - 6\\hat{j} - 13\\hat{k} N m\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q51 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q51: Show that the moment of a couple is independent of the choice of the origin or reference point.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> Consider two opposite forces <b>F</b> at position <b>r</b>_1 and -<b>F</b> at position <b>r</b>_2 with respect to origin O.\n      <br/>Total torque about O:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      <b>&tau;</b> = <b>r</b>_1 &times; <b>F</b> + <b>r</b>_2 &times; (-<b>F</b>) = (<b>r</b>_1 - <b>r</b>_2) &times; <b>F</b>\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      Here, (<b>r</b>_1 - <b>r</b>_2) = <b>r</b>_{12} is the relative position vector connecting the two points of application, which is completely independent of the choice of reference origin O.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q52 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q52: A uniform meter stick of mass M is balanced horizontally on a knife-edge at the 40 cm mark when a mass of 20 g is suspended at the 10 cm mark. Determine the mass M of the meter stick.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> The meter stick has its centre of mass at the 50 cm mark. Fulcrum is at 40 cm mark.\n      <br/>Effort/suspended mass: m = 20 g at 10 cm; distance from fulcrum d_1 = 40 - 10 = 30 cm.\n      <br/>Meter stick mass M acts at 50 cm; distance from fulcrum d_2 = 50 - 40 = 10 cm.\n      <br/>Applying the Principle of Moments:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      Anticlockwise Torque = Clockwise Torque &rArr; 20 &times; 30 = M &times; 10<br/>\n      M = (600 / 10) = 60 g\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q53 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q53: Can a body be in translational equilibrium without being in rotational equilibrium? Give an example.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> <b>Yes.</b> When a couple acts on a body (e.g., turning a steering wheel or opening a water tap with two equal and opposite forces), \\sum <b>F</b> = <b>F</b> - <b>F</b> = <b>0</b>, so the body is in translational equilibrium. However, the net torque \\sum <b>&tau;</b> = F d \\ne 0, so it is not in rotational equilibrium and accelerates angularly.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q54 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q54: A particle of mass 2 kg moves with a constant velocity v = 5&icirc; m/s along the straight line y = 4 m. Calculate its angular momentum about the origin.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> At any time, <b>r</b> = x\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j}, and linear momentum <b>p</b> = m<b>v</b> = 2(5\\hat{i}) = 10\\hat{i} kg m/s.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      <b>L</b> = <b>r</b> &times; <b>p</b> = (x\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j}) &times; (10\\hat{i}) = 40(\\hat{j} &times; \\hat{i}) = -40\\hat{k} kg m<sup>2</sup>/s\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      The angular momentum has constant magnitude <b>40 J s</b> directed along -\\hat{k}, independent of x and time t.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q55 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q55: A uniform circular disc of mass 4 kg and radius 0.5 m rotates about its central axis at 120 rpm. Calculate its angular momentum.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> \n      <br/>Angular frequency: \\omega = (120 &times; 2&pi; / 60) = 4&pi; &approx; 12.57 rad/s.\n      <br/>Moment of inertia: I = (1 / 2) M R<sup>2</sup> = (1 / 2)(4)(0.5)<sup>2</sup> = 0.5 kg m<sup>2</sup>.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      L = I \\omega = 0.5 &times; 4&pi; = 2&pi; &approx; 6.28 J s\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q56 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q56: Explain how a falling cat manages to land on its feet without violating the conservation of angular momentum.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> Even though net external torque is zero (\u000bec{L} = \u000bec{0}), a cat arches its back and twists its front and rear halves in opposite directions. By pulling its front paws in (low I) and extending its rear legs (high I), it rotates the front by a large angle while the rear rotates in reverse by only a small angle. Repeating this sequence in reverse re-orients its feet downward with zero net angular momentum.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q57 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q57: If the Earth suddenly contracts to half its present radius without change in mass, calculate the new duration of the day.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> For a solid sphere, I = (2 / 5) M R<sup>2</sup> &prop; R<sup>2</sup>.\n      <br/>Since no external torque acts, L = I \\omega = constant &rArr; I_1 ((2&pi; / T_1)) = I_2 ((2&pi; / T_2)).\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      T_2 = T_1 ((I_2 / I_1)) = T_1 ((R_2 / R_1))<sup>2</sup> = 24 &times; ((1 / 2))<sup>2</sup> = 24 &times; (1 / 4) = 6 hours\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q58 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q58: A ballet dancer spins at 2 rev/s with outstretched arms. When she folds her arms, her moment of inertia decreases by 40%. Find her new rotational speed.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> Initial speed f_1 = 2 rev/s, initial moment of inertia I_1.\n      <br/>Final moment of inertia I_2 = I_1 - 0.40 I_1 = 0.60 I_1.\n      <br/>By conservation of angular momentum:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      I_1 f_1 = I_2 f_2 &rArr; f_2 = (I_1 / I_2) f_1 = (I_1 / 0.60 I_1)(2) = (2 / 0.6) = (10 / 3) &approx; 3.33 rev/s\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q59 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q59: A flywheel decelerates uniformly from 600 rpm to 300 rpm in 10 seconds under a braking torque. Calculate its angular acceleration and the number of revolutions made in this interval.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> \n      <br/>\\omega_0 = (600 &times; 2&pi; / 60) = 20&pi; rad/s, \\omega = (300 &times; 2&pi; / 60) = 10&pi; rad/s, t = 10 s.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      \\alpha = (\\omega - \\omega_0 / t) = (10&pi; - 20&pi; / 10) = -&pi; &approx; -3.14 rad/s<sup>2</sup><br/>\n      &theta; = ((\\omega_0 + \\omega / 2)) t = ((20&pi; + 10&pi; / 2)) &times; 10 = 150&pi; rad<br/>\n      Revolutions  N = (&theta; / 2&pi;) = (150&pi; / 2&pi;) = 75 revolutions\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q60 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q60: An electric fan rotating at 900 rpm is switched off and completes 200 revolutions before stopping. Find its constant angular deceleration and the time taken to halt.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> \n      <br/>\\omega_0 = (900 &times; 2&pi; / 60) = 30&pi; rad/s, \\omega = 0, &theta; = 200 &times; 2&pi; = 400&pi; rad.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      \\omega<sup>2</sup> = \\omega<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> + 2\\alpha &theta; &rArr; 0 = (30&pi;)<sup>2</sup> + 2\\alpha(400&pi;)<br/>\n      \\alpha = -(900&pi;<sup>2</sup> / 800&pi;) = -(9&pi; / 8) &approx; -3.53 rad/s<sup>2</sup><br/>\n      t = (\\omega - \\omega_0 / \\alpha) = (-30&pi; / -9&pi;/8) = (240 / 9) = (80 / 3) &approx; 26.67 s\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q61 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q61: Show that for a body consisting of n equal-mass particles, the radius of gyration is the root-mean-square distance from the rotational axis.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> Let body have n particles, each of mass m, so total mass M = n m.\n      <br/>Moment of inertia: I = m r_1<sup>2</sup> + m r_2<sup>2</sup> + \\dots + m r_n<sup>2</sup> = m(r_1<sup>2</sup> + r_2<sup>2</sup> + \\dots + r_n<sup>2</sup>).\n      <br/>By definition of radius of gyration, I = M k<sup>2</sup> = (n m) k<sup>2</sup>:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      (n m) k<sup>2</sup> = m(r_1<sup>2</sup> + r_2<sup>2</sup> + \\dots + r_n<sup>2</sup>) &rArr; k = &radic;{(r_1<sup>2</sup> + r_2<sup>2</sup> + \\dots + r_n<sup>2</sup> / n)}}\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      Thus, k is precisely the <b>root-mean-square (RMS)</b> distance of the particles from the axis of rotation.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q62 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q62: Find the ratio of the radius of gyration of a circular ring to that of a circular disc of the same radius about their central perpendicular axes.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> \n      <br/>&bull; For circular ring: I_{ring} = M R<sup>2</sup> = M k_{ring}<sup>2</sup> &rArr; k_{ring} = R.\n      <br/>&bull; For circular disc: I_{disc} = (1 / 2) M R<sup>2</sup> = M k_{disc}<sup>2</sup> &rArr; k_{disc} = (R / &radic;(2)).\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      \frac{k_{ring}}{k_{disc}} = (R / R / &radic;(2)) = &radic;(2) : 1 &approx; 1.414 : 1\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q63 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q63: Calculate the moment of inertia and radius of gyration of a uniform solid cylinder of mass 5 kg and radius 0.2 m about its central cylindrical axis.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> For a solid cylinder, I = (1 / 2) M R<sup>2</sup>.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      I = (1 / 2)(5)(0.2)<sup>2</sup> = 2.5 &times; 0.04 = 0.1 kg m<sup>2</sup><br/>\n      k = &radic;{(I / M)}} = &radic;{(0.1 / 5)}} = &radic;(0.02) &approx; 0.1414 m\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q64 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q64: Three point masses each of mass m are fixed at the vertices of an equilateral triangle of side a. Calculate the moment of inertia of the system about an axis passing through one vertex perpendicular to the plane.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> Let the axis pass through vertex A.\n      <br/>Distance of mass at A from axis: r_A = 0.\n      <br/>Distance of mass at B from axis: r_B = a.\n      <br/>Distance of mass at C from axis: r_C = a.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      I = m r_A<sup>2</sup> + m r_B<sup>2</sup> + m r_C<sup>2</sup> = m(0)<sup>2</sup> + m(a<sup>2</sup>) + m(a<sup>2</sup>) = 2 m a<sup>2</sup>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q65 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q65: A thin uniform rod of length 1.2 m and mass 2 kg rotates about an axis perpendicular to its length through one of its ends. Calculate its moment of inertia and radius of gyration.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> For an axis through one end:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      I = (1 / 3) M L<sup>2</sup> = (1 / 3)(2)(1.2)<sup>2</sup> = (2 &times; 1.44 / 3) = 0.96 kg m<sup>2</sup><br/>\n      k = (L / &radic;(3)) = (1.2 / &radic;(3)) &approx; 0.693 m\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q66 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q66: Calculate the rotational kinetic energy of a solid sphere of mass 10 kg and radius 0.2 m rotating about its diameter at 30 rad/s.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> \n      <br/>Moment of inertia: I = (2 / 5) M R<sup>2</sup> = (2 / 5)(10)(0.2)<sup>2</sup> = 4 &times; 0.04 = 0.16 kg m<sup>2</sup>.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      K_{rot} = (1 / 2) I \\omega<sup>2</sup> = (1 / 2)(0.16)(30)<sup>2</sup> = 0.08 &times; 900 = 72 Joules\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q67 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q67: Compare linear momentum and angular momentum in terms of formula, SI unit, and conservation conditions.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> \n      <br/>&bull; <b>Linear Momentum (\u000bec{P}):</b> Formula \u000bec{P} = m\u000bec{v}, SI Unit: \text{kg m s}<sup>-1</sup>, Conserved when sum \u000bec{F}_{\text{ext}} = \u000bec{0}.\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Angular Momentum (\u000bec{L}):</b> Formula \u000bec{L} = \u000bec{r}\times\u000bec{p} = I\u000bec{omega}, SI Unit: \text{J s} (or \text{kg m}<sup>2</sup>\text{ s}<sup>-1</sup>), Conserved when sum \u000bec{\tau}_{\text{ext}} = \u000bec{0}.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q68 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q68: Compare Force and Torque in rotational dynamics.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b>\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Force (\u000bec{F}):</b> Causes linear acceleration (\u000bec{F} = m\u000bec{a}); work done dW = \u000bec{F} cdot d\u000bec{r}; unit is Newton (N).\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Torque (\u000bec{\tau}):</b> Causes angular acceleration (\u000bec{\tau} = I\u000bec{alpha}); work done dW = \u000bec{\tau} cdot d\u000bec{\theta}; unit is Newton-metre (N m).\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q69 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q69: State the Theorem of Parallel Axes.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> The moment of inertia I of any rigid body about an arbitrary axis is equal to its moment of inertia I_{cm} about a parallel axis passing through its centre of mass plus the product of total mass M and the square of perpendicular distance d between the two axes:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      I = I_{cm} + M d<sup>2</sup>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q70 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q70: State the Theorem of Perpendicular Axes for a planar lamina.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> For a flat planar lamina lying in the xy-plane, the moment of inertia I_z about an axis perpendicular to the plane is equal to the sum of its moments of inertia about two mutually perpendicular axes x and y lying in its plane and intersecting at the same point:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      I_z = I_x + I_y &bull; (Valid exclusively for 2D thin laminar bodies)\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q71 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q71: Use the perpendicular axis theorem to find the moment of inertia of a circular disc about any diameter.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> For a planar circular disc, the perpendicular central axis has I_z = (1 / 2) M R<sup>2</sup>.\n      <br/>By symmetry, all diametrical axes are identical: I_x = I_y = I_{dia}.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      I_z = I_x + I_y = 2 I_{dia} &rArr; I_{dia} = (I_z / 2) = (1 / 4) M R<sup>2</sup>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q72 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q72: Calculate the moment of inertia of a thin circular ring of mass M and radius R about a tangent in its plane.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> \n      <br/>About a diameter in the plane: I_{dia} = (1 / 2) M R<sup>2</sup> (from I_z = M R<sup>2</sup> = 2 I_{dia}).\n      <br/>The tangent in the plane is parallel to the diameter at distance d = R. By Parallel Axis Theorem:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      I_{tangent} = I_{dia} + M R<sup>2</sup> = (1 / 2) M R<sup>2</sup> + M R<sup>2</sup> = (3 / 2) M R<sup>2</sup>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q73 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q73: A constant torque of 200 N m is applied to a flywheel of moment of inertia 40 kg m<sup>2</sup> initially at rest. Find its angular velocity after 5 seconds and work done by the torque.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> \n      <br/>Angular acceleration: \\alpha = (&tau; / I) = (200 / 40) = 5 rad/s<sup>2</sup>.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      \\omega = \\omega_0 + \\alpha t = 0 + 5(5) = 25 rad/s<br/>\n      W = &Delta;K_{rot} = (1 / 2) I \\omega<sup>2</sup> = (1 / 2)(40)(25)<sup>2</sup> = 20 &times; 625 = 12,500 Joules (12.5 kJ)\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q74 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q74: A solid sphere and a hollow sphere of identical mass and radius roll down the same incline without slipping. Which reaches the bottom first?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> The linear acceleration of a body rolling down an incline is a = (g\\sin&theta; / 1 + k<sup>2</sup>/R<sup>2</sup>).\n      <br/>&bull; For solid sphere: k<sup>2</sup>/R<sup>2</sup> = 2/5 = 0.40 &rArr; a_s = (g\\sin&theta; / 1.4) &approx; 0.714 g\\sin&theta;.\n      <br/>&bull; For hollow sphere: k<sup>2</sup>/R<sup>2</sup> = 2/3 &approx; 0.67 &rArr; a_h = (g\\sin&theta; / 1.67) &approx; 0.60 g\\sin&theta;.\n    </p>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      Since a_s > a_h, the <b>solid sphere</b> accelerates faster and reaches the bottom first.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q75 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q75: Derive the mathematical expression for the work done by a torque &tau; in rotating a body through an angle &theta;.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> A tangential force F acts at radius r, displacing the point through arc ds = r\\,d&theta;.\n      <br/>Work done: dW = F\\,ds = F(r\\,d&theta;) = (F r)\\,d&theta; = &tau;\\,d&theta;.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      W = \\int_0<sup>&theta;</sup> &tau;\\,d&theta; &bull; (For constant torque: W = &tau; &theta;)\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q76 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q76: Show that the rotational power delivered by a torque is P = &tau; &omega;.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> Instantaneous power is the rate of doing work:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      P = (dW / dt) = \frac{&tau;\\,d&theta;', 'dt')} = &tau; ((d&theta; / dt)) = &tau; \\omega\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      This is the exact rotational analogue of linear power P = F v.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q77 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q77: A cord is wound around the rim of a flywheel of diameter 0.4 m and moment of inertia 0.5 kg m<sup>2</sup>. A steady pull of 25 N is exerted on the cord. Find the angular acceleration of the wheel.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> Radius R = 0.4 / 2 = 0.2 m.\n      <br/>Torque exerted: &tau; = F &times; R = 25 &times; 0.2 = 5 N m.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      \\alpha = (&tau; / I) = (5 / 0.5) = 10 rad/s<sup>2</sup>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q78 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q78: Explain why a massive flywheel is attached to the crankshaft of internal combustion engines.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> In a 4-stroke engine, power is delivered only during the brief power stroke, while the other 3 strokes consume energy. A massive flywheel possesses a very large moment of inertia I. It absorbs excess kinetic energy during the power stroke with negligible speed increase (Delta omega = Delta K / Iomega), and delivers it back during the exhaust, suction, and compression strokes, maintaining a remarkably smooth rotational speed.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q79 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q79: A wheel of moment of inertia 2 kg m<sup>2</sup> rotating at 30 rad/s is brought into contact with a stationary wheel of moment of inertia 4 kg m<sup>2</sup> on the same axis. Find the common angular speed and energy lost.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> By Conservation of Angular Momentum:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      I_1 \\omega_1 = (I_1 + I_2) \\omega &rArr; \\omega = (2 &times; 30 / 2 + 4) = (60 / 6) = 10 rad/s<br/>\n      K_i = (1 / 2)(2)(30)<sup>2</sup> = 900 J<br/>\n      K_f = (1 / 2)(6)(10)<sup>2</sup> = 300 J<br/>\n      &Delta;K_{loss} = 900 - 300 = 600 Joules (lost as heat and frictional sound).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q80 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q80: Why is a tail rotor indispensable on single-rotor helicopters? Explain using conservation of angular momentum.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Solution:</b> By Newton&apos;s third law and conservation of angular momentum (L_{total} = 0), when the helicopter&apos;s engine drives the main overhead rotor blade in a clockwise direction, an equal and opposite reaction torque is exerted on the fuselage, causing the body of the helicopter to spin uncontrollably in the counter-clockwise direction. The vertical <b>tail rotor</b> exerts a sideways horizontal thrust at a long lever arm from the mast, generating a counter-torque that cancels the reaction torque and stabilizes the fuselage.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <!-- SECTION C: LONG ANSWER (LA) QUESTIONS & DERIVATIONS (5 MARKS EACH) -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.08); border-left: 4px solid #AB47BC; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 25px 0 15px 0; border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      SECTION C: Long Answer (LA) Questions & Derivations (5 Marks Each) &bull; Q81 to Q100\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 13px;\">\n      Exhaustive mathematical derivations, calculus integrations, equilibrium systems, rotational dynamics proofs, and board exam derivations.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q81 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q81: Derive the expressions for the position, velocity, and acceleration of the centre of mass of a two-particle system. Prove that internal forces cancel out.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Derivation:</b>\n      <br/>Consider two particles of masses m_1 and m_2 located at position vectors <b>r</b>_1 and <b>r</b>_2 with respect to an origin O.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 1: Position Vector of CM</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\"><b>R</b>_{cm} = \frac{m_1 <b>r</b>_1 + m_2 <b>r</b>_2}{m_1 + m_2} = (1 / M)(m_1 <b>r</b>_1 + m_2 <b>r</b>_2) where M = m_1 + m_2.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 2: Velocity of Centre of Mass</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Differentiating with respect to time t:\n        <b>V</b>_{cm} = \frac{d<b>R</b>_{cm}}{dt} = (1 / M)(m_1 \frac{d<b>r</b>_1}{dt} + m_2 \frac{d<b>r</b>_2}{dt}) = \frac{m_1 <b>v</b>_1 + m_2 <b>v</b>_2}{M}\n        Multiplying by M: M <b>V</b>_{cm} = m_1 <b>v</b>_1 + m_2 <b>v</b>_2 = <b>p</b>_1 + <b>p</b>_2 = <b>P</b>_{total}.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 3: Acceleration of Centre of Mass & Newton&apos;s Second Law</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Differentiating once more with respect to t:\n        <b>A</b>_{cm} = \frac{d<b>V</b>_{cm}}{dt} = (1 / M)(m_1 <b>a</b>_1 + m_2 <b>a</b>_2) &rArr; M <b>A</b>_{cm} = m_1 <b>a</b>_1 + m_2 <b>a</b>_2\n        The total force on m_1 is <b>F</b>_1 = <b>F</b>_{1,ext} + <b>F</b>_{12}, and on m_2 is <b>F</b>_2 = <b>F</b>_{2,ext} + <b>F</b>_{21}.\n        M <b>A</b>_{cm} = (<b>F</b>_{1,ext} + <b>F</b>_{12}) + (<b>F</b>_{2,ext} + <b>F</b>_{21}) = (<b>F</b>_{1,ext} + <b>F</b>_{2,ext}) + (<b>F</b>_{12} + <b>F</b>_{21})</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 4: Cancellation of Internal Forces by Newton&apos;s Third Law</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">By Newton&apos;s Third Law, <b>F</b>_{12} = -<b>F</b>_{21} &rArr; <b>F</b>_{12} + <b>F</b>_{21} = <b>0</b>.\n        M <b>A</b>_{cm} = <b>F</b>_{1,ext} + <b>F</b>_{2,ext} = <b>F</b>_{ext}\n        <b>A</b>_{cm} = \frac{<b>F</b>_{ext}}{M}\n        <b>Conclusion:</b> The centre of mass accelerates solely in response to external forces; internal interaction forces cancel out completely.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q82 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q82: Prove that in the absence of external forces, the centre of mass moves with constant velocity. Discuss its application to an exploding projectile.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Proof:</b> From the equation of motion of the centre of mass:\n      M <b>A</b>_{cm} = <b>F</b>_{ext}.\n      <br/>If no external force acts on the system, <b>F</b>_{ext} = <b>0</b>:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin: 8px 0;\">\n      M \frac{d<b>V</b>_{cm}}{dt} = <b>0</b> &rArr; <b>V</b>_{cm} = constant vector &rArr; <b>R</b>_{cm}(t) = <b>R</b>_0 + <b>V</b>_{cm} t\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Application to Exploding Projectile:</b>\n      <br/>When a shell is fired at angle \theta with speed u, it follows a standard parabolic trajectory under gravity (M<b>g</b>). If it explodes in mid-air at the highest point into multiple fragments:\n      <br/>&bull; The explosive blast is generated purely by <b>internal chemical forces</b>, which sum to zero.\n      <br/>&bull; The only external force acting on the collection of flying fragments is still the constant gravitational force: <b>F</b>_{ext} = \\sum m_i <b>g</b> = M<b>g</b>.\n      <br/>&bull; Therefore, the acceleration of the centre of mass remains <b>A</b>_{cm} = <b>g</b> downwards.\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Result:</b> The centre of mass of the fragments continues precisely along the original parabolic trajectory until the first fragment strikes the ground.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q83 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q83: Rigorously derive the position of the centre of mass of a thin uniform rod of length L and mass M using calculus integration.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Derivation:</b>\n      <br/>Let a thin uniform rod of mass M and length L lie along the positive x-axis with its left end at the origin x = 0 and right end at x = L.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 1: Linear Mass Density & Differential Mass Element</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Since the rod is uniform, its linear mass density &lambda; is constant:\n        &lambda; = (M / L)\n        Consider an infinitesimal element of length dx situated at distance x from the origin.\n        dm = &lambda;\\,dx = (M / L)\\,dx</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 2: Integration of Mass Moment</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">By definition of the centre of mass for continuous mass distributions:\n        X_{cm} = (1 / M)\\int x\\,dm = (1 / M)&int;<sub>0</sub><sup>L</sup> x ((M / L)\\,dx) = (1 / L)&int;<sub>0</sub><sup>L</sup> x\\,dx</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 3: Evaluating the Definite Integral</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">&int;<sub>0</sub><sup>L</sup> x\\,dx = [ (x<sup>2</sup> / 2) ]<sub>0</sub><sup>L</sup> = (L<sup>2</sup> / 2) - 0 = (L<sup>2</sup> / 2)\n        Substituting back:\n        X_{cm} = (1 / L) &times; (L<sup>2</sup> / 2) = (L / 2)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 4: Conclusion & Physical Interpretation</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Since the rod has uniform cross-section, Y_{cm} = 0 and Z_{cm} = 0.\n        Therefore, the centre of mass is situated exactly at the <b>geometric midpoint</b>:\n        <b>R</b>_{cm} = ((L / 2), 0, 0)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q84 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q84: Define torque (moment of a force). Derive its Cartesian rectangular components and explain the Right-Hand Grip Rule.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Definition:</b> Torque (moment of a force) <b>&tau;</b> represents the turning or rotational effect produced by a force <b>F</b> acting at position <b>r</b> relative to a reference pivot:\n      <b>&tau;</b> = <b>r</b> &times; <b>F</b>.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 1: Cartesian Representation of Vectors</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Let <b>r</b> = x\\hat{i} + y\\hat{j} + z\\hat{k} and <b>F</b> = F_x\\hat{i} + F_y\\hat{j} + F_z\\hat{k}.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 2: Determinant Cross Product Expansion</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\"><b>&tau;</b> = <b>r</b> &times; <b>F</b> = \\begin{vmatrix} \\hat{i} & \\hat{j} & \\hat{k} \\\\ x & y & z \\\\ F_x & F_y & F_z \\end{vmatrix}\n        <b>&tau;</b> = \\hat{i}(y F_z - z F_y) + \\hat{j}(z F_x - x F_z) + \\hat{k}(x F_y - y F_x)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 3: Component Identification</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">\\tau_x = y F_z - z F_y\n        \\tau_y = z F_x - x F_z\n        \\tau_z = x F_y - y F_x\n        For planar motion in the xy-plane (z = 0, F_z = 0), only the z-component survives: \\tau_z = x F_y - y F_x.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 4: Direction via Right-Hand Grip Rule</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Point the fingers of the right hand along <b>r</b> and curl them towards <b>F</b> through the smaller angle &theta;. The extended thumb indicates the unique axial vector direction of <b>&tau;</b>. Counter-clockwise rotation yields torque pointing outward (+z); clockwise rotation yields torque pointing inward (-z).</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q85 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q85: Derive the master relation between torque and angular momentum: &tau;&vec; = dL&vec;/dt for a single particle and extend it to a system of particles.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Derivation:</b>\n      <br/>By definition, the angular momentum of a particle of mass m with position vector <b>r</b> and linear momentum <b>p</b> is:\n      <b>L</b> = <b>r</b> &times; <b>p</b>.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 1: Differentiating with Product Rule</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">(d<b>L</b> / dt) = (d / dt)(<b>r</b> &times; <b>p</b>) = ((d<b>r</b> / dt) &times; <b>p</b>) + (<b>r</b> &times; (d<b>p</b> / dt))</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 2: Vanishing of the First Term</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Note that (d<b>r</b> / dt) = <b>v</b> and <b>p</b> = m<b>v</b>.\n        (d<b>r</b> / dt) &times; <b>p</b> = <b>v</b> &times; (m<b>v</b>) = m(<b>v</b> &times; <b>v</b>) = <b>0</b>\n        because the cross product of any vector with itself is identically zero.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 3: Substitution of Newton&apos;s Second Law</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">By Newton&apos;s Second Law, (d<b>p</b> / dt) = <b>F</b>.\n        Therefore:\n        (d<b>L</b> / dt) = <b>0</b> + <b>r</b> &times; <b>F</b> = <b>r</b> &times; <b>F</b> = <b>&tau;</b></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 4: Extension to System of Particles</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">For a system: <b>L</b>_{sys} = \\sum <b>L</b>_i.\n        {d<b>L</b>_{sys}}{dt} = \\sum (d<b>L</b>_i / dt) = \\sum (<b>&tau;</b>_{i,ext} + <b>&tau;</b>_{i,int})\n        Internal torques between pairs of particles cancel in pairs by Newton&apos;s third law (central forces).\n        <b>&tau;</b>_{ext} = {d<b>L</b>_{sys}}{dt}</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q86 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q86: State and derive the Law of Conservation of Angular Momentum. Explain two real-world applications in detail.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Statement:</b> When the net external torque acting on a system of particles or a rotating rigid body is zero, the total angular momentum of the system remains constant in magnitude and direction.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Mathematical Proof</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">From the fundamental rotational dynamical equation:\n        <b>&tau;</b>_{ext} = (d<b>L</b> / dt)\n        If <b>&tau;</b>_{ext} = <b>0</b>:\n        (d<b>L</b> / dt) = <b>0</b> &rArr; <b>L</b> = constant vector\n        For rotation about a fixed principal axis where L = I\\omega:\n        I \\omega = constant &rArr; I_1 \\omega_1 = I_2 \\omega_2</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 10px 0 0 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Real-World Applications:</b>\n      <br/><b>1. Ballet Dancers and Figure Skaters:</b> A skater initiates a pirouette with arms and one leg stretched outward, creating a large moment of inertia I_1. When she pulls her arms and leg tightly against her torso, her moment of inertia decreases dramatically to I_2 < I_1. Since I_1 omega_1 = I_2 omega_2, her spin velocity omega_2 accelerates sharply into a rapid spin.\n      <br/><b>2. Acrobatic Springboard Divers:</b> On launching from the board, a diver leaves the board with a fixed angular momentum. In mid-air, the diver draws the knees to the chest and tucks the head (decreasing I), enabling 3 to 4 complete somersaults. Just prior to hitting the water, the diver fully extends arms and legs (increasing I), dropping omega to zero for a clean, vertical entry.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q87 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q87: Prove Kepler&apos;s Second Law of Planetary Motion using the conservation of angular momentum under a central gravitational force.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Proof:</b>\n      <br/>Let a planet of mass m orbit the Sun of mass M_s located at the origin O.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 1: Central Force & Torque Calculation</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">The gravitational pull of the Sun is a central force directed along -\\hat{r}:\n        <b>F</b> = -(G M_s m / r<sup>2</sup>)\\,\\hat{r}\n        The torque acting on the planet about the Sun:\n        <b>&tau;</b> = <b>r</b> &times; <b>F</b> = <b>r</b> &times; (-(G M_s m / r<sup>2</sup>)\\,\\hat{r}) = <b>0</b>\n        since <b>r</b> is parallel to \\hat{r} (\theta = 180\\circ, \\sin 180\\circ = 0).</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 2: Conservation of Angular Momentum</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\"><b>&tau;</b> = (d<b>L</b> / dt) = <b>0</b> &rArr; <b>L</b> = <b>r</b> &times; <b>p</b> = constant vector</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 3: Geometrical Area Swept Out in Time dt</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">In differential time dt, the position vector <b>r</b> moves through displacement d<b>r</b> = <b>v</b>\\,dt.\n        The infinitesimal triangular area swept out by <b>r</b> is:\n        d<b>A</b> = (1 / 2)(<b>r</b> &times; d<b>r</b>) = (1 / 2)(<b>r</b> &times; <b>v</b>\\,dt)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 4: Areal Velocity Formula</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Dividing both sides by dt:\n        (d<b>A</b> / dt) = (1 / 2)(<b>r</b> &times; <b>v</b>) = (1 / 2m)(<b>r</b> &times; m<b>v</b>) = \frac{<b>L</b>}{2m}\n        Since <b>L</b> and m are constant, the <b>areal velocity</b> is constant:\n        (dA / dt) = (L / 2m) = constant\n        This proves Kepler&apos;s second law: the radius vector sweeps out equal areas in equal intervals of time.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q88 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q88: Formulate the conditions for complete mechanical equilibrium of a rigid body and establish the Principle of Moments.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Theory of Equilibrium:</b> A rigid body is in complete mechanical equilibrium when its linear momentum and angular momentum are both constant in time.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">1. First Condition: Translational Equilibrium</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\"><b>P</b> = constant &rArr; (d<b>P</b> / dt) = <b>0</b> &rArr; \\sum <b>F</b>_{ext} = <b>0</b>\n        In Cartesian components:\n        \\sum F_x = 0, \\quad \\sum F_y = 0, \\quad \\sum F_z = 0</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">2. Second Condition: Rotational Equilibrium</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\"><b>L</b> = constant &rArr; (d<b>L</b> / dt) = <b>0</b> &rArr; \\sum <b>&tau;</b>_{ext} = <b>0</b>\n        In Cartesian components about any arbitrary point O:\n        \\sum \\tau_x = 0, \\quad \\sum \\tau_y = 0, \\quad \\sum \\tau_z = 0</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">3. Derivation of Principle of Moments for a Lever</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Consider a light horizontal beam supported at fulcrum F. A load W = F_1 acts downward at distance d_1 to the left, and an effort E = F_2 acts downward at distance d_2 to the right. Normal reaction at fulcrum is R.\n        Translational balance: R - F_1 - F_2 = 0 &rArr; R = F_1 + F_2.\n        Rotational balance taking torques about fulcrum F:\n        \\sum &tau; = F_1 d_1 - F_2 d_2 = 0 &rArr; F_1 d_1 = F_2 d_2\n        Load &times; Load Arm = Effort &times; Effort Arm\n        Mechanical Advantage: M.A. = (F_1 / F_2) = (d_2 / d_1).</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q89 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q89: Derive the three equations of rotational motion with constant angular acceleration &alpha; using the calculus method.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Calculus Derivation:</b>\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">1. First Equation: &omega; = &omega;<sub>0</sub> + &alpha; t</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">By definition, angular acceleration \\alpha = (d\\omega / dt) &rArr; d\\omega = \\alpha\\,dt.\n        Integrating between limits t = 0 (where \\omega = \\omega_0) to t = t (where \\omega = \\omega):\n        \\int_{\\omega_0}\\omega d\\omega = \\alpha &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> dt &rArr; \\omega - \\omega_0 = \\alpha t &rArr; \\omega = \\omega_0 + \\alpha t</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">2. Second Equation: &theta; = &omega;<sub>0</sub> t + &frac12; &alpha; t<sup>2</sup></div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">By definition, \\omega = (d&theta; / dt) &rArr; d&theta; = \\omega\\,dt = (\\omega_0 + \\alpha t)\\,dt.\n        Integrating between t = 0 (where &theta; = 0) to t = t (where &theta; = &theta;):\n        &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>&theta;</sup> d&theta; = &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> (\\omega_0 + \\alpha t)\\,dt = \\omega_0 &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> dt + \\alpha &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> t\\,dt\n        &theta; = \\omega_0 t + (1 / 2)\\alpha t<sup>2</sup></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">3. Third Equation: &omega;<sup>2</sup> = &omega;<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> + 2&alpha;&theta;</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Using the chain rule: \\alpha = (d\\omega / dt) = (d\\omega / d&theta;) (d&theta; / dt) = \\omega (d\\omega / d&theta;) &rArr; \\omega\\,d\\omega = \\alpha\\,d&theta;.\n        Integrating between \\omega_0 to \\omega and 0 to &theta;:\n        \\int_{\\omega_0}\\omega \\omega\\,d\\omega = \\alpha &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>&theta;</sup> d&theta; &rArr; [ (\\omega<sup>2</sup> / 2) ]_{\\omega_0}\\omega = \\alpha &theta;\n        (\\omega<sup>2</sup> - \\omega<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> / 2) = \\alpha &theta; &rArr; \\omega<sup>2</sup> = \\omega<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> + 2\\alpha&theta;$</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q90 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q90: Derive the formula for Rotational Kinetic Energy: K<sub>rot</sub> = &frac12; I &omega;<sup>2</sup> and prove the Rotational Work-Energy Theorem.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Derivation:</b>\n      <br/>Consider a rigid body rotating about a fixed axis with angular velocity omega.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 1: Summing Kinetic Energies of Constituent Particles</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Let the body comprise n particles of masses m_1, m_2, \\dots, m_n at perpendicular distances r_1, r_2, \\dots, r_n from the axis of rotation.\n        The linear speed of the i-th particle is v_i = r_i \\omega.\n        The total kinetic energy of the rigid body is:\n        K_{rot} = \\sum_{i=1}<sup>n</sup> (1 / 2) m_i v_i<sup>2</sup> = \\sum_{i=1}<sup>n</sup> (1 / 2) m_i (r_i \\omega)<sup>2</sup> = (1 / 2)(\\sum_{i=1}<sup>n</sup> m_i r_i<sup>2</sup>) \\omega<sup>2</sup></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 2: Introducing Moment of Inertia I</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">By definition, I = \\sum_{i=1}<sup>n</sup> m_i r_i<sup>2</sup>.\n        K_{rot} = (1 / 2) I \\omega<sup>2</sup></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 3: Rotational Work-Energy Theorem Proof</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Work done by torque in infinitesimal angular displacement d&theta; is dW = &tau;\\,d&theta;.\n        Using &tau; = I\\alpha = I((d\\omega / dt)) and d&theta; = \\omega\\,dt:\n        dW = (I (d\\omega / dt))(\\omega\\,dt) = I \\omega\\,d\\omega\n        Integrating from initial angular speed \\omega_0 to final angular speed \\omega:\n        W = \\int_{\\omega_0}\\omega I\\omega\\,d\\omega = I [ (\\omega<sup>2</sup> / 2) ]_{\\omega_0}\\omega = (1 / 2) I \\omega<sup>2</sup> - (1 / 2) I \\omega<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> = &Delta;K_{rot}\n        <b>Conclusion:</b> Work done by external torque equals the change in rotational kinetic energy.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q91 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q91: Define Moment of Inertia and Radius of Gyration. Explain why Moment of Inertia is neither a true scalar nor a simple vector (Tensor nature).</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Moment of Inertia (I):</b> Defined mathematically as I = \\sum m_i r_i<sup>2</sup>. Physically, it represents the resistance of a rigid body to angular acceleration under applied torque (\tau = Ialpha).\n      <br/><b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Radius of Gyration (k):</b> The perpendicular distance from the axis of rotation to a single point where, if the entire mass M of the body were concentrated, its moment of inertia would remain identical: I = M k<sup>2</sup> implies k = sqrt{I/M}.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 4px; margin: 10px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Why Moment of Inertia is a Tensor:</b>\n      <br/>1. <b>Not a Scalar:</b> A scalar possesses only magnitude and has the exact same value in all directions. For a rigid body (e.g. an ellipsoid or a cylinder), the moment of inertia changes completely when calculated about different axes passing through the very same point (I_x \ne I_y \ne I_z).\n      <br/>2. <b>Not a Vector:</b> It does not obey the parallelogram law of vector addition and has no unique direction in space.\n      <br/>3. <b>Tensor of Rank 2:</b> An applied torque vector \u000bec{\tau} along one axis can produce angular acceleration \u000bec{alpha} along a different axis unless the rotation is about a principal axis of inertia. In general, \u000bec{L} = \\mathbf{I} <b>\\omega</b>, where \\mathbf{I} is a 3 &times; 3 symmetric matrix (the Inertia Tensor).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q92 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q92: Construct an exhaustive comparison between Linear Translational Mechanics and Rotational Mechanics.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Tabular Correspondence:</b>\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"overflow-x: auto;\">\n      <table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 13px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n        <thead>\n          <tr style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC;\">\n            <th style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); text-align: left;\">Feature / Concept</th>\n            <th style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); text-align: left;\">Translational Motion</th>\n            <th style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); text-align: left;\">Rotational Motion</th>\n          </tr>\n        </thead>\n        <tbody>\n          <tr>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Displacement</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">s or \u000bec{r} (m)</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">\theta (rad)</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Velocity</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">v = ds/dt (m/s)</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">omega = d\theta/dt (rad/s)</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Acceleration</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">a = dv/dt (m/s<sup>2</sup>)</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">alpha = domega/dt (rad/s<sup>2</sup>)</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Inertia Measure</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Mass M (kg)</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Moment of Inertia I = sum m_i r_i<sup>2</sup> (kg m<sup>2</sup>)</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Dynamical Cause</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Force \u000bec{F} (N)</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Torque \u000bec{\tau} = \u000bec{r}\times\u000bec{F} (N m)</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Newton&apos;s 2nd Law</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">F = M a</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">\tau = I alpha</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Momentum</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">P = M v</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">L = I omega</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Kinetic Energy</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">K = \frac{1}{2} M v<sup>2</sup></td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">K_{\text{rot}} = \frac{1}{2} I omega<sup>2</sup></td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Work Done</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">W = int F,ds</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">W = int \tau,d\theta</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Power</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">P = F v</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">P = \tau omega</td>\n          </tr>\n        </tbody>\n      </table>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q93 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q93: Analyze pure rolling motion without slipping. Derive the total kinetic energy formula: K = &frac12; M v<sub>cm</sub><sup>2</sup> (1 + k<sup>2</sup>/R<sup>2</sup>).</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Derivation:</b>\n      <br/>Pure rolling of a body of mass M, radius R, and radius of gyration k is the simultaneous combination of translation of the centre of mass and rotation about the centre of mass.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 1: Condition for Rolling Without Slipping</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">At the instantaneous point of contact with the ground, the backward tangential velocity due to rotation must cancel the forward translational velocity:\n        v_{contact} = v_{cm} - R\\omega = 0 &rArr; v_{cm} = R\\omega &rArr; \\omega = \frac{v_{cm}}{R}</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 2: Total Kinetic Energy Expression</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">K_{total} = K_{trans} + K_{rot} = (1 / 2) M v_{cm}<sup>2</sup> + (1 / 2) I_{cm} \\omega<sup>2</sup></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 3: Factoring with Radius of Gyration k</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Substitute I_{cm} = M k<sup>2</sup> and \\omega = {v_{cm}}{R}:\n        K_{total} = (1 / 2) M v_{cm}<sup>2</sup> + (1 / 2)(M k<sup>2</sup>)(\frac{v_{cm}}{R})<sup>2</sup>\n        K_{total} = (1 / 2) M v_{cm}<sup>2</sup> + (1 / 2) M v_{cm}<sup>2</sup> ((k<sup>2</sup> / R<sup>2</sup>))\n        K_{total} = (1 / 2) M v_{cm}<sup>2</sup> (1 + (k<sup>2</sup> / R<sup>2</sup>))</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 4: Energy Partition Ratios</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Translational fraction: {K_{trans}}{K_{total}} = (1 / 1 + k<sup>2</sup>/R<sup>2</sup>)<br/>\n        Rotational fraction: {K_{rot}}{K_{total}} = (k<sup>2</sup>/R<sup>2</sup> / 1 + k<sup>2</sup>/R<sup>2</sup>)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q94 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q94: Derive the acceleration of a body rolling down a rough incline without slipping. Rank ring, disc, and sphere in an incline race.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Derivation:</b>\n      <br/>Consider a round body of mass M, radius R, radius of gyration k rolling down an incline of angle \theta.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 1: Equations of Motion</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Translational equation down the slope:\n        M g \\sin&theta; - f_s = M a\n        Rotational equation about centre of mass:\n        &tau; = f_s R = I \\alpha = (M k<sup>2</sup>) ((a / R)) &rArr; f_s = M a ((k<sup>2</sup> / R<sup>2</sup>))</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 2: Solving for Linear Acceleration a</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Substitute f_s into the translational equation:\n        M g \\sin&theta; - M a ((k<sup>2</sup> / R<sup>2</sup>)) = M a\n        g \\sin&theta; = a (1 + (k<sup>2</sup> / R<sup>2</sup>)) &rArr; a = (g\\sin&theta; / 1 + )(k<sup>2</sup> / R<sup>2</sup>)}}</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 3: Minimum Friction Coefficient for Pure Rolling</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Normal force N = M g \\cos&theta;. For no slipping: f_s \\le \\mu_s N.\n        M a ((k<sup>2</sup> / R<sup>2</sup>)) \\le \\mu_s M g \\cos&theta; &rArr; \\mu_s \\ge (\\tan&theta; / 1 + )(R<sup>2</sup> / k<sup>2</sup>)}}</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 4: Race Down the Incline (Ring vs Disc vs Solid Sphere)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Values of k<sup>2</sup>/R<sup>2</sup>:\n        <br/>&bull; Solid Sphere: k<sup>2</sup>/R<sup>2</sup> = 2/5 = 0.40 &rArr; a = (5 / 7) g\\sin&theta; &approx; 0.714 g\\sin&theta;\n        <br/>&bull; Solid Disc: k<sup>2</sup>/R<sup>2</sup> = 1/2 = 0.50 &rArr; a = (2 / 3) g\\sin&theta; &approx; 0.667 g\\sin&theta;\n        <br/>&bull; Ring: k<sup>2</sup>/R<sup>2</sup> = 1.00 &rArr; a = (1 / 2) g\\sin&theta; = 0.500 g\\sin&theta;\n        <br/><b>Winner:</b> The <b>solid sphere</b> arrives first, followed by the <b>disc</b>, and the <b>ring</b> finishes last.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q95 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q95: Two masses m<sub>1</sub> and m<sub>2</sub> (m<sub>1</sub> > m<sub>2</sub>) are connected by a string over a massive pulley of moment of inertia I. Derive system acceleration and string tensions.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Derivation:</b>\n      <br/>Let the pulley have mass M, radius R, moment of inertia I, and let string tensions on the two sides be T_1 and T_2 (T_1 \ne T_2 because torque is required to turn the massive pulley).\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 1: Free Body Equations of Suspended Masses</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">For descending mass m_1: m_1 g - T_1 = m_1 a &rArr; T_1 = m_1(g - a)<br/>\n        For ascending mass m_2: T_2 - m_2 g = m_2 a &rArr; T_2 = m_2(g + a)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 2: Rotational Dynamics of the Pulley</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Net torque on the pulley: &tau; = (T_1 - T_2) R = I \\alpha = I((a / R))\n        T_1 - T_2 = (I a / R<sup>2</sup>)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Step 3: Calculating Acceleration a</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Substitute expressions for T_1 and T_2:\n        m_1(g - a) - m_2(g + a) = (I a / R<sup>2</sup>)\n        (m_1 - m_2)g = a (m_1 + m_2 + (I / R<sup>2</sup>))\n        a = ((m_1 - m_2)g / m_1 + m_2 + I/R<sup>2</sup>)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q96 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q96: A person on a frictionless turntable pulls dumbbells inward. Show that kinetic energy increases and identify the origin of this energy.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Analysis:</b>\n      <br/>Let initial moment of inertia and angular velocity be I_1 and omega_1. When dumbbells are pulled in, moment of inertia decreases to I_2 < I_1.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Conservation of Angular Momentum</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Since no external torque acts about the vertical axis:\n        L = I_1 \\omega_1 = I_2 \\omega_2 = constant &rArr; \\omega_2 = ((I_1 / I_2)) \\omega_1 > \\omega_1</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Ratio of Final to Initial Kinetic Energy</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Express kinetic energy in terms of angular momentum: K = (L<sup>2</sup> / 2I).\n        (K_2 / K_1) = (L<sup>2</sup> / (2I_2) / L<sup>2</sup> / (2I_1)) = (I_1 / I_2)\n        Since I_1 > I_2, (K_2 / K_1) > 1 &rArr; K_2 > K_1.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Origin of Additional Kinetic Energy</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">The extra rotational kinetic energy is not created out of nothing; it is supplied directly by the <b>internal biological work done by the person&apos;s muscular forces</b> against centrifugal inertia while pulling the heavy weights inwards toward the rotation axis.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q97 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q97: A uniform ladder of mass M and length L leans at angle &theta; to the horizontal against a smooth wall. Derive the minimum coefficient of static friction &mu;<sub>s</sub> with the floor to prevent slipping.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Derivation:</b>\n      <br/>The ladder makes angle \theta with the rough ground. Weight M g acts downward at midpoint (L/2). Smooth vertical wall exerts normal force N_w horizontally. Floor exerts vertical normal force N_f and horizontal static friction force f_s.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Translational Equilibrium Equations</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Vertical: \\sum F_y = 0 &rArr; N_f = M g<br/>\n        Horizontal: \\sum F_x = 0 &rArr; f_s = N_w</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Rotational Equilibrium About Base of Ladder</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Taking torques about the contact point on the floor:\n        \\sum &tau; = (M g)((L / 2)\\cos&theta;) - N_w(L\\sin&theta;) = 0\n        N_w = (M g \\cos&theta; / 2\\sin&theta;) = (M g / 2\\tan&theta;)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Condition for No Slipping</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Static friction must satisfy f_s \\le \\mu_s N_f:\n        (M g / 2\\tan&theta;) \\le \\mu_s (M g) &rArr; \\mu_s \\ge (1 / 2\\tan&theta;) = (1 / 2)\\cot&theta;$\n        <b>Minimum Coefficient of Friction:</b> \\mu_{s,\\min} = (1 / 2\\tan&theta;).</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q98 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q98: A block of height h and base b rests on a rough floor. A horizontal force F is applied at height y. Derive conditions for sliding vs toppling.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Analysis:</b>\n      <br/>Let block have mass M, resting on floor with static friction coefficient mu_s.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Condition for Sliding</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">The block begins sliding when the applied force exceeds maximum static friction:\n        F_{slide} = f_{s,\\max} = \\mu_s M g</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Condition for Toppling</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Toppling occurs about the front edge when the overturning torque of F exceeds the restoring torque of gravity:\n        \\tau_{overturning} = F y, \\quad \\tau_{restoring} = M g ((b / 2))\n        F_{topple} = (M g b / 2 y)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.2); color: #AB47BC; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #AB47BC;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #AB47BC; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Comparison: Sliding vs Toppling</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">&bull; <b>Block slides before toppling</b> if F_{slide} < F_{topple} &rArr; \\mu_s < (b / 2y).<br/>\n        &bull; <b>Block topples before sliding</b> if F_{topple} < F_{slide} &rArr; \\mu_s > (b / 2y).</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q99 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q99: Describe the operational principles of flywheels and gyroscopes. Explain gyroscopic stability and flywheel energy storage.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">1. Flywheel Energy Storage Systems:</b>\n      <br/>A flywheel is a heavy wheel with large moment of inertia I designed to store kinetic energy (K_{\text{rot}} = \frac{1}{2}Iomega<sup>2</sup>). Modern flywheels made of carbon-fiber composites rotate at upwards of 60,000 rpm in vacuum chambers on magnetic levitation bearings. They deliver rapid surge power and store regenerative braking energy in electric trains and power grids with efficiency exceeding 90%.\n      <br/><br/>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">2. Gyroscopic Precession & Stability:</b>\n      <br/>A gyroscope consists of a rapidly spinning rotor possessing large angular momentum \u000bec{L}.\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Gyroscopic Inertia (Rigidity in Space):</b> In the absence of external torque, \u000bec{L} maintains its orientation fixed in inertial space, providing an drift-free directional reference for aircraft and spacecraft navigation (inertial guidance systems).\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Precession:</b> When an external torque \u000bec{\tau} is applied perpendicular to \u000bec{L}, the angular momentum vector changes according to d\u000bec{L} = \u000bec{\tau},dt. Rather than falling over, the spin axis slowly rotates (precesses) in a horizontal circle with precession angular velocity:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #AB47BC; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin: 8px 0;\">\n      \\Omega_p = (&tau; / L) = (&tau; / I \\omega)\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q100 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(171, 71, 188, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #AB47BC; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q100: Master Synthesis of Unit V: System of Particles and Rotational Motion. Summarize the core mathematical laws, moments of inertia, and examination traps.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Summary of Core Dynamical Principles:</b>\n      <br/>1. <b>Centre of Mass:</b> \u000bec{R}_{\text{cm}} = \frac{1}{M}sum m_i \u000bec{r}_i, \u000bec{F}_{\text{ext}} = M\u000bec{A}_{\text{cm}}. Internal forces cancel identically.\n      <br/>2. <b>Torque & Angular Momentum:</b> \u000bec{\tau} = \u000bec{r}\times\u000bec{F}, \u000bec{L} = \u000bec{r}\times\u000bec{p} = I\u000bec{omega}, and \u000bec{\tau}_{\text{ext}} = \frac{d\u000bec{L}}{dt}.\n      <br/>3. <b>Equilibrium:</b> Both conditions must simultaneously hold: sum\u000bec{F}_{\text{ext}} = \u000bec{0} and sum\u000bec{\tau}_{\text{ext}} = \u000bec{0}.\n      <br/>4. <b>Pure Rolling:</b> v_{\text{cm}} = Romega, K_{\text{total}} = \frac{1}{2}M v_{\text{cm}}<sup>2</sup>(1 + k<sup>2</sup>/R<sup>2</sup>), and acceleration on an incline a = \frac{gsin\theta}{1 + k<sup>2</sup>/R<sup>2</sup>}.\n      <br/><br/>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Standard Moments of Inertia Reference Table:</b>\n      <br/>&bull; Circular Ring (M, R) about central axis: I = M R<sup>2</sup> (k = R)\n      <br/>&bull; Circular Disc (M, R) about central axis: I = \frac{1}{2} M R<sup>2</sup> (k = R/sqrt{2})\n      <br/>&bull; Thin Rod (M, L) about centre: I = \frac{1}{12} M L<sup>2</sup> &bull; about end: I = \frac{1}{3} M L<sup>2</sup>\n      <br/>&bull; Solid Cylinder (M, R) about axis: I = \frac{1}{2} M R<sup>2</sup>\n      <br/>&bull; Hollow Cylinder (M, R) about axis: I = M R<sup>2</sup>\n      <br/>&bull; Solid Sphere (M, R) about diameter: I = \frac{2}{5} M R<sup>2</sup>\n      <br/>&bull; Spherical Shell (M, R) about diameter: I = \frac{2}{3} M R<sup>2</sup>\n      <br/><br/>\n      <b style=\"color: #AB47BC;\">Common Board Exam Traps:</b>\n      <br/>&bull; Confusing torque and work: Both have dimensions [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>], but torque is an axial vector cross product (N m) and work is a scalar dot product (J).\n      <br/>&bull; Incline race order: The body with the <b>smallest k<sup>2</sup>/R<sup>2</sup>$</b> wins the race (Solid Sphere > Disc > Hollow Sphere > Ring).\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n\n</div>";

